// Generated by npm run build:docs. Admin-only API; not under public/.
export const RELEASE_DOCS=[{"name":"V18-1-1-PUBLIC-ANNOUNCEMENTS-TELEMETRY-REFRESH.md","content":"# WASP v18.1.1 — Public announcements and local telemetry refresh\n\nAn additive refinement on **v18.1.0**, with no destructive migrations or changes to Docker volume mappings, Cloudflare D1/R2 bindings, user playlists, music or HIVE peer identities. Install on Test before Main. The older `/api/user/announcements` endpoint remains available to older signed-in clients; the new public client uses `/api/announcements`.\n\n## Announcements for everyone, not just registered users\n\nUnder **Admin → Users → Announcements**, the admin still chooses a template, edits a required title and message, chooses a severity and opacity (50–98%), sees the live preview and presses Send. A new **server-local public** read-only API, `GET /api/announcements`, shares the last 20 announcements from the preceding 30 days without user data. No account, session, browser ID or peer federation is required. Only the admin may create messages through `POST /api/admin/announcements`. Messages are never distributed to other HIVE servers.\n\nThe player checks the public feed every **two seconds while its tab is visible**, and immediately when the tab regains focus or network connectivity. A shared two-second edge cache reduces repeated Cloudflare D1 reads when many visitors are present. This is **near-live polling, not a guaranteed instantaneous WebSocket push**: delivery may take several seconds depending on edge caching, network availability and browser throttling. No background push is delivered after the page is closed.\n\nThe notice appears near the lower-left corner above the player for up to 30 seconds; visitors can dismiss it sooner. The origin-scoped browser cache records a notice as handled **only after dismissal or completion of its display**, not when the notice first appears. Refreshing the page during its 30-second display resumes the notice rather than making it vanish. Later/new messages are shown even after earlier ones are handled. An announced notice may preempt an earlier one; the earlier notice resumes with its remaining display time. A small bell/music-note control on the notification lets visitors mute or enable the optional ding, saved locally in the browser.\n\n### Optional notification ding\n\nA quiet, original Web Audio two-tone beep (~170 milliseconds) is played when a notification is displayed **only when the browser permits sound**. Browsers typically require the visitor to interact with the page first. The beep has an independent low-gain audio graph; it does not pause music, alter the music volume or play tracks. The sound can be muted per browser. If the browser blocks autoplay, announcements still appear silently.\n\n## Update current telemetry\n\nUnder **Admin → Network → Telemetry**, next to the selected-peer pull controls, click **Update current telemetry**. The admin-only `POST /api/admin/telemetry/recalculate` invalidates this server's cached telemetry summaries and metadata-coverage snapshot, then reports the **current local** and **Entire HIVE** confirmed-play counts from the existing retained event ledger.\n\nThis button **does not** pull remote events, record fake listens, enrich music, modify origin IDs, or delete history. Use **Pull telemetry from selected peers now** to import remote events, and **Update current telemetry** to rebuild calculations from events already on this WASP server. The HIVE event uniqueness rule still deduplicates imported plays, and scheduled synchronization remains under Crons.\n\n## Verification and limitations\n\nRun `node --test tests/*.test.mjs`, `python tests/ui_v18_1_1_public_announcements.py`, `python tests/ui_v18_1_0_server_communications.py`, and the existing Radio, star ratings, metadata and player continuity browser tests. These tests run on isolated databases/mocked browser APIs; they cannot guarantee real Cloudflare/Docker production behavior or network latency. Test sending an announcement from an admin session to a guest/private browsing session on the same WASP domain before rolling to Main. Verify the notice appears, persists across refresh until handled, can be dismissed, and that a second announcement arrives after the first. Check that a different WASP domain does not show it.\n\n> No WebSocket or Durable Object binding is introduced. Real sub-second broadcast to browsers across all Cloudflare POPs would require a stateful push/broker service and further deployment configuration.\n"},{"name":"V18-1-0-SERVER-IDENTITY-TELEMETRY-ANNOUNCEMENTS.md","content":"# WASP v18.1.0 — Server identity, selective HIVE telemetry and local announcements\n\nThis is an additive refinement built **only on v18.0.9**. Upgrade on Test first, preserve existing Cloudflare D1/R2 bindings and Docker state volumes, and never use `docker compose down -v`. Existing music, playlists, users, history, hearts, ratings and request data are retained. No background action is performed merely by opening the admin page.\n\n## Server name vs browser tab name\n\nUnder **Admin → General settings → Branding**, **Server name** still controls the top-left text (or label when using a custom image). It is also the name announced to administrator-connected HIVE peers via `/api/peer`. **Browser tab name** changes the HTML document title for listeners browsing this particular server; it is not used as its identity in the connected peers list. Existing settings keys (`site_name`, `tab_title`) are preserved, so no migration is required for branding. The peer handshake's historical `title` alias now mirrors the server name to avoid advertising the local browser title to other administrators. As with earlier versions, the public `/api/site` endpoint still returns the browser title so local browsers can display it; it is not intended as a secret.\n\n## Selective manual telemetry pull\n\nUnder **Admin → Network → Telemetry → Telemetry**, click **Select all peers** or check specific **administrator-connected** peers, then **Pull telemetry from selected peers now**. A refresh button reloads peer names and last-known states. The UI shows import counts, failures and progress. More than 10 selected peers are processed in sequential bounded groups; an interrupted run can safely be retried. The API `/api/admin/telemetry/peers` lists only managed peer URLs to an authenticated administrator. `/api/admin/telemetry/pull` accepts a validated array of 1–10 managed peer URLs per invocation; it cannot be used to fetch arbitrary internet URLs or community-submitted endpoints.\n\nThe existing `telemetry_events` ledger keeps the source's `origin_id` and `event_id` uniqueness constraint. Imported events contribute to **Entire HIVE** totals, including those also imported through another peer, without becoming duplicate plays. Imported events do not change the **This server** scope. Newly imported events invalidate cached telemetry summaries so a manual dashboard refresh shows updated numbers. Peers must be online and allow telemetry sharing to supply feeds. Historic partial feeds, inaccessible peers and events beyond the source retention limit cannot be filled in automatically.\n\nThe existing **Network → Crons** settings are separate: the telemetry sharing checkbox controls public feed exposure and automatic scheduled sync. Manual pulls do not change the cron schedule or the peer's sharing settings. The current HIVE model is a peer-exposed read feed plus receiver-initiated pull; the crons UI calls this “PUSH / sharing” for convenience, not a direct transfer initiated by the source.\n\n## Local announcements to registered users\n\nUnder **Admin → Users → Announcements**, admins can send a **required title** (up to 100 characters) and **required message** (up to 2,000 characters) to registered users on **this server only**. Messages do not appear in peer catalogue, telemetry or metadata feeds and are never replicated through HIVE. The message creation page contains six editable templates (planned downtime, new uploads, requests being fulfilled, feature update, service restored, urgent notice), a theme-aware severity choice, a 50–98% opacity slider, and a **live preview** that updates while typing. Confirm the Send action to save a new message to the server's local database. Previous messages can be reviewed in the admin panel.\n\nWhen a signed-in user opens their account, WASP checks for recent announcements; the visible app checks again roughly once a minute while in use. A new message appears above the player at the lower-left corner for **30 seconds**, or until the user closes it. Each browser saves the IDs of displayed messages in `localStorage` under a username-scoped key, so a normal reload or app update does not replay announcements that have already been viewed or dismissed. Other browsers or devices may show the same announcement independently, and clearing browser data may make it reappear. The server retains announcements up to the most recent 30 days for user delivery; the admin list retains the most recent 30 by creation date. Newly registered users do not receive notices sent before account creation. Guests and administrators do not receive the account-targeted overlay.\n\nAnnouncements are plain text: HTML and scripts are not executed. This feature adds a single additive `server_announcements` D1/local database table and does not modify music or account records. When sending multiple notices, each is queued for display rather than overlaying the previous one.\n\n## Testing\n\n```bash\nnpm test\npython tests/ui_v18_1_0_server_communications.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_9_radio_wheel.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_8_star_rankings.py\npython tests/ui_v18_features.py\nnpm run build:docs\n```\n\nThe automated tests use isolated SQLite and in-memory Chromium mocks; they do **not** prove that production Cloudflare Worker/D1/R2, Docker, Nginx bridge or live peers work identically. On Test WASP, verify actual user logins, cross-peer import counts, the new announcement display, and browser cache behavior before updating Main. D1 reads/writes from manual pulls and announcement polling are still subject to Cloudflare account limits.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-9-RADIO-GENRE-WHEEL-PAGING.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.9 — Radio Genre Spacing & Wheel Paging\n\nThis small UI-only release is built on **v18.0.8** and changes `/#radio` genre selection without changing HIVE matching, playlists, playback, metadata, ratings or stored data.\n\n- Remove the unintended 16px right margin inherited from the shared `fieldset label` rule. Genre pills now keep an intentional 6px gap, closely grouped without merging visually.\n- Scrolling the mouse wheel vertically **or horizontally** over the genre-pill area switches one page at a time. Down/right advances; up/left returns.\n- Limit rapid trackpad momentum to at most one page per 450ms, so a single scroll gesture doesn't overshoot several pages.\n- Retain the clickable page dots, genre selections and removable chips across page changes. At either boundary, normal outer-page scrolling remains available.\n- Keep checkbox accessibility (visually hidden, keyboard focusable); desktop and mobile continue to work.\n\nNo database migration, Docker volume change, Cloudflare binding change or new dependency is required. Deploy to Test WASP first, verify mouse-wheel and touchpad behavior, then update Main WASP. Never remove persistent Docker volumes.\n\n## Verification\n\n```bash\nnpm test\npython tests/ui_v18_0_9_radio_wheel.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_7_radio_stars.py\n```\n\nAutomated tests are isolated; they do not certify a live AWS/Cloudflare deployment.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-8-MOST-STARRED-RANKINGS.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.8 — Most starred rankings and voting UI polish\n\nThis **staging update** builds on the exact v18.0.7 baseline and corrects the navigation and five-star voting presentation. It does not retag or delete any uploaded music.\n\n## What changed\n\n- Restores the former **Most liked** placement in the public sidebar, directly below **Most hearted**, as **Most starred** (`/#stars`). Its icon is the existing user-provided `star.png`. Legacy `/#likes` and `/#dislikes` links redirect to `/#stars` rather than showing misleading thumb counts.\n- Adds **Songs**, **Artists** and **Playlists** tabs on the star-based rankings page. The page uses the existing lazy-loaded telemetry/ranking document, so opening it does not interrupt the persistent audio player.\n- Rankings use **actual `star_votes` records** and sort by **total stars awarded**, then number of voters and average rating. Each card displays total stars, average rating out of five, and voter count. Voting a new score replaces a visitor's previous vote rather than creating another vote.\n- The ranking API returns aggregated counts **without voter identities**. Private account playlists and unregistered peer identities are excluded from public rankings. Peer music only appears in the browser when its catalogue metadata is available locally.\n- Removes the **extra decorative star image beside the five selectable stars** on song, artist and playlist voting controls. The heart and five independent clickable stars remain.\n- Updates the example home-featured slider link from old thumbs-up rankings to the new star rankings.\n\n## Storage and compatibility\n\nThis is a non-destructive UI and read-only rankings endpoint update. No existing hearts, ratings, historical likes/dislikes, playlists, audio files or telemetry are deleted or converted. The `star_votes` table added in v18.0.7 remains the source of truth. New installations return an empty ranking until the first star vote; legacy thumb votes are **not** silently counted as stars.\n\n## Testing\n\nTests include public ranking order, vote replacement, legacy-reaction separation, private-playlist filtering, unregistered peer filtering, desktop and mobile navigation, artist/playlist tabs, absence of decorative voting icons, and preserved player/radio behavior. Chromium browser checks run in-memory and do not exercise production Cloudflare or AWS. Deploy to a test Worker and verify real D1/R2/peer behavior before updating production.\n\nKeep the existing storage bindings and Docker volumes. Do not use `docker compose down -v`.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-7-HIVE-PICARD-RATINGS-REQUEST-REVIEW.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.7 — Picard metadata, HIVE ratings and request review\n\nBuilt on the exact v18.0.6 HIVE Radio UI baseline. **This is a staging release; do not treat synthetic browser or SQLite tests as production certification.** Preserve Docker volumes, R2 objects and D1/local database snapshots before upgrading. No existing audio file is rewritten or deleted by the features below.\n\n## Embedded metadata, using actual MusicBrainz Picard MP3 samples\n\nWASP's bounded MP3 tag reader and browser upload reader now extract more of the existing ID3v2 fields:\n\n- Main release date (`TDRC`, year or `YYYY-MM-DD`), original release (`TDOR`, `TXXX:originaldate`/`originalyear`) and original-year-first classification for **Music by Decade**. Remasters retain both dates rather than conflating the edition with the original release.\n- Credited artist list (`TXXX:ARTISTS`) and aligned MusicBrainz artist UUIDs (`TXXX:MusicBrainz Artist Id`). A collaborator is not silently merged with the main artist.\n- Genre (`TCON`), title, album and track number; preserved embedded artwork continues to use the existing artwork path.\n- Explicit artist-country codes (`TXXX:artistcountry`) and begin area (`TXXX:artistbeginarea`) when present. Never infer artist origin from `MusicBrainz Album Release Country` (`XW` means worldwide market).\n- Timestamped text inside `USLT` is recognized as *timed lyrics metadata*, distinct from a proper `SYLT` frame; this version does **not** introduce a karaoke lyrics renderer.\n- Embedded `POPM` ratings are recognized for metadata inspection, but the new *WASP vote* system stays independent; uploading a file cannot submit a listener's vote.\n\nThe two real supplied files were tested separately (without bundling copyrighted MP3 files): *Angel of My Dreams* (JADE) and *AFRAID TO DIE* (P.O.D. feat. Tatiana Shmayluk). Both include original release dates, genre, artwork, MusicBrainz artist IDs and timestamped `USLT`; neither includes an explicit artist-origin country or `POPM`. Their release-country value is a market, not origin.\n\nDocker upgrades perform one durable read-only backfill pass on existing web-uploaded MP3 objects, even if year/genre were already populated. Unchanged folder files are tracked by the persistent incremental index with tag-reader versioning. Verified country values are shareable through the existing trusted metadata feed. For artists with a MusicBrainz ID but no country tag, the Docker offline index can disambiguate matching artist identities where the database contains the matching ID; it will not invent a country if lookup is ambiguous or absent.\n\n## Public HIVE Radio genre paging and Up Next\n\n- Compact selectable genre pills (no visible checkboxes), colored lime when active; selections remain active across pages and appear as individually removable chips.\n- Dot indicators navigate horizontal *pages* of 24 genres; no tall scrollbar. Responsive desktop/mobile layouts.\n- In Up Next, a playlist name is clickable and navigates to the existing playlist route when its source ID is known.\n- Ephemeral Radio queues, song limits, guest access and account-save flow are unchanged.\n\n## Hearts + five-star WASP ratings\n\n- Existing hearts stay available.\n- Track, artist and playlist reaction controls show five star positions; clicking a star records a 1–5 score for that visitor or account, clicking the same star again clears their vote.\n- Both server aggregates and the visitor's own rating are returned. Source-specific peer identifiers prevent accidental cross-server mixing.\n- Thumbs and the Most Liked / Most Disliked public navigation items are hidden. Legacy reactions remain **stored**, so no historical reaction data is destroyed.\n- The provided `star.png` is included as decorative artwork. New ratings **do not** backfill existing thumbs votes or import a Picard `POPM` rating as a site-wide vote.\n\n## Admin → Network → Telemetry & Metadata\n\nFour subtabs organize the same saved settings: **Offline Metadata**, **Online Metadata**, **Telemetry**, **Others**. The global Save settings action stays visible regardless of active subtab. Offline matching requires a complete Docker index; online lookup has a separate explicit action. Telemetry contains PULL/PUSH explanation and links to the existing Crons controls. All existing provider credentials remain server-side.\n\n## Requested Spotify playlist review\n\nAn admin can inspect missing Spotify entries, scan fuzzy *suggestions* from the local/peer catalogues and manually search tracks. Suggestions are never automatically accepted. The red **❌** rejects a candidate and the green **✅** explicitly confirms the match. Source selection is validated server-side before being saved. Song entries may also be added, temporarily removed (and restored), or marked as done with explicit warnings when some songs are unavailable or Spotify supplied only a partial list. The list of known missing songs survives in the completed request metadata; the created playlist contains only matched entries.\n\nThis feature manages the **local WASP's playlist requests**. A remote HIVE request still belongs to its originating peer and cannot be rewritten or force-completed from another server. Local completion does not delete any original MP3.\n\n## Staging checks\n\n```bash\nnpm test\npython tests/ui_v18_0_7_review.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_7_radio_stars.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_6_radio_layout.py\npython tests/ui_v18_0_4_offline.py\n```\n\nFor a live test server, confirm D1 migration, R2 tag reads, the Docker file backfill, trusted peer exchange, account permissions, Spotify embed completeness, playlists with 100+ entries and Up Next navigation. Full production-scale MusicBrainz downloads and real Cloudflare/Docker execution are not included in these isolated tests.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-6-HIVE-RADIO-UI-POLISH.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.6 — HIVE Radio Interface Polish\n\nThis update refines the public `/#radio` form without changing how songs are selected, played, or saved.\n\n- **Artist:** A capped, accessible list of matching artists appears **directly below the search field** instead of in a browser-controlled popup beside the search box. Search by partial name and choose with a click or with the Arrow/Enter keys. Escape dismisses suggestions. The current text entry remains available.\n- **Genre:** Each checked genre appears as a chip beside the Artist / Genre / Decade / Top songs controls. Press its **×** button to uncheck and exclude that genre immediately, without resetting the form or already-generated radio.\n- **Artist and Decade:** Their inputs align horizontally with **Songs in radio**, the optional **Custom number** field, and **Generate radio** on desktop. Hint text appears below its own control without shifting the other fields down. Narrow screens stack the controls.\n\nThe existing ephemeral queue, HIVE matching, 1–100 track cap, anonymous access and signed-in playlist saving remain unchanged. No database migration or persistent data changes are required.\n\n## Testing\n\n```bash\nnpm test\npython tests/ui_v18_0_6_radio_layout.py\n```\n\nThe browser tests run against isolated fixture data and are not a substitute for staging-server validation.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-5-HIVE-RADIO-PLAYLIST-UX.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.5 — Public HIVE Radio & Playlist UI Refinement\n\nThis staging update builds **only** on v18.0.4. Back up your D1/R2 or Docker `.wrangler/state`, uploads, music mappings, playlists and settings before upgrading. The optional v18.0.4 MusicBrainz offline index is unchanged. Existing audio files remain untouched.\n\n## Public HIVE Radio (`/#radio`)\n\nThe **Radio** navigation link appears between **Playlists** and **Most hearted**, using the supplied `public/nav-icons/radio.png`. All visitors can generate browser-only playback queues. Radio is independent of login and does not save rows, playlists or duplicate audio when generated.\n\n- **Artist:** Start with that artist and add available songs from similar artists (including supported genre relationships), without filling from unrelated performers when fewer songs are found.\n- **Genre:** Check multiple genre tags, generating songs that match at least one selection.\n- **Decade:** Match known 1850+ release years in a selected decade. Unknown years are excluded, never estimated.\n- **Top songs:** Rank by all-time confirmed, deduplicated HIVE telemetry, not local upload count; only play songs that still exist in this installation's reachable catalogue. The top-100 aggregate is cached for five minutes.\n- **Quantity:** 20, 30, 50, or custom 1–100. If fewer tracks qualify, show the actual number, notify the user and suggest contributing a server through the supported account publishing workflow.\n- **Playback:** The full generated list is visible on `/#radio` and enters the persistent player / Up next queue. It survives navigation within the current page session but is not a permanent playlist.\n- **Save as playlist:** Logged-in *member* accounts can register eligible HIVE peer references and save a new private playlist using existing ownership checks. Guests see a registration/login prompt. No audio is copied or deleted.\n\n## Browsing and editor improvements\n\n- Center Week/Month/Year/All time controls in each Telemetry tile.\n- Move staged **Undo** and **Save changes** directly above the owned-playlist track list, not beside its cover.\n- Render title, album, credited artists, verified year, available genre and duration in wide song rows. Smaller devices hide less important columns and keep artist attribution beneath the title.\n- The bottom player artist credit is an accessible link. Single-artist playback opens the artist details; multiple credited performers navigate to `/#artists` with a comma-separated multi-name filter. This filter is not enabled in other sections.\n- Review potential playlist duplicates by title similarity and artist overlap, with a separate edition marker to avoid conflating live/remix variants. The owner must explicitly choose a candidate to remove; Undo remains available (five-step history), and the server is untouched until Save. Only playlist references are updated — no music files are deleted.\n\n## Compatibility and security\n\nThe Radio page is public but uses no private member data. `GET /api/radio/top` exposes anonymous aggregate telemetry only and shares a bounded summary cache. `/api/tracks` supplies release years and genre labels only when present in established metadata tables, gracefully falling back to older installations. Existing HIVE routing, Cloudflare Workers, Docker and optional Mobile Bridge behavior remain in place. No database schema destructive migration is introduced.\n\n## Staging verification checklist\n\n1. `npm test` and `python tests/ui_v18_0_5_radio.py` on the source package.\n2. Confirm the Radio icon renders in both expanded and collapsed sidebars, guest generation works, and Spotify / other browsing functions are unaffected.\n3. Verify decade/genre/artist/top lists respect available songs, unavailable peers, missing years, and the 100-song limit.\n4. Verify guest save prompts login and authenticated member save creates exactly one private playlist (and preserves peer track references).\n5. Review duplicate suggestions in a real 300+ track playlist. Stage Remove → Undo → Save and check that no audio changes and the original source remains intact.\n6. Verify center alignment and metadata columns at desktop, tablet and mobile widths, and verify player artist links for one and multiple artists.\n7. On Docker, verify build, existing HTTP+HTTPS mappings and mounted volumes; on Cloudflare, verify available D1 quota and new cached aggregate endpoint.\n\n**Limitation:** Matching and generating radio relies on available metadata. Some songs in a large Spotify/HIVE catalogue may lack release year or genre, so these modes can produce fewer than requested until metadata enrichment completes.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-4-HIVE-OFFLINE-METADATA-ENGINE.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.4 — HIVE Offline Metadata Engine\n\nStaging upgrade from exact v18.0.3. No schema rebuild, audio edits or volume renames. The optional Docker feature indexes official MusicBrainz JSONL artist and release-group data in a separate SQLite file within the existing persistent local-state volume. Cloudflare continues using the existing HIVE metadata feed; it does not download the dataset. The Docker image adds Python 3 (standard library only); SQLite lookup uses Node 22's built-in SQLite module.\n\n## Initial deployment and storage\n\n1. Back up the existing WASP Docker state volume and R2/database as appropriate. Deploy the full source or overlay patch from v18.0.3. Rebuild the Docker image.\n2. Check `df -h` on Docker storage. Recommend 15–20 GiB of **free** disk before downloading (including headroom for index updates); more may be needed depending on snapshot size. The installer refuses to begin with less than 10 GiB free, checks free space periodically and tries to preserve a 2 GiB reserve. This is a protective threshold, not a guarantee that the full dataset fits. Download and indexing sizes are not yet benchmarked with a full live snapshot.\n3. Docker WASP → Admin → Network → Telemetry → HIVE Offline Metadata Engine → **Download / update MusicBrainz**. The first download is opt-in. Official JSON dumps are several GB; the browser may close while the job runs. Progress is per download / indexing phase, not a single guessed overall percentage. Cancel stops the background build without deleting the current installed index.\n4. After the index is ready, click **Scan missing metadata** or resume the existing scanner. A completed continuous scan automatically detects new snapshot versions and new identities. Uncheck **Use configured online providers for unresolved metadata after offline matching** and save for offline-only factual enrichment while the index is installed. Disable the optional remote MP3 tag reads if focusing on the fastest offline factual pass; keep trusted peer metadata enabled if useful. For missing genres or unresolved tracks, re-enable fallback afterwards. An unavailable or not-yet-installed offline engine uses the existing provider workflow; you can disable the providers or pause the scan if desired.\n5. Optional: set `WASP_OFFLINE_AUTO_UPDATE=1` in Portainer stack environment and redeploy to check for a newer official snapshot roughly weekly after an initial installation. Default is manual updates only. Manual download/update checks the current snapshot and skips a redundant download. An interrupted first download is retried from the start, not resumed byte-for-byte.\n6. Cloudflare WASP → Admin → Network → Telemetry → trust administrator-connected peers, choose Docker as preferred, disable scheduled external provider enrichment if conserving resources, and pull metadata from HIVE. Docker must be reachable and metadata sharing enabled. Imported facts still require D1 reads/writes and the existing feed is paginated.\n\n## Data source and matching\n\nOfficial source: https://ftp.musicbrainz.org/pub/musicbrainz/data/json-dumps/ (LATEST, SHA256SUMS, artist.tar.xz and release-group.tar.xz only). See https://metabrainz.org/datasets/postgres-dumps and https://musicbrainz.org/doc/About/Data_License. Core facts from MusicBrainz are CC0. No audio, supplementary ratings/tags, biographies or artwork are indexed. Downloads use HTTPS and the published SHA-256 checksum manifest; GPG signature verification is not implemented.\n\nThe builder streams the compressed tar member (does not unpack arbitrary files), retains only matching keys, MBID, artist country and album first-release year, then builds search indexes. It validates the SQLite database before atomically replacing the previous index. The complete database is never transferred to Cloudflare. Data lives in `/app/.wrangler/state/hive-offline/index.sqlite`; database builds use separate temporary files, not the WASP database. A full update rebuilds the index, not the music library.\n\nExact normalized artist and album-credit/name matches are required. Multiple same-name identities, missing dates/countries, conflicting embedded years, and unknown artists/albums are left unresolved. Album dates refer to the release group (not necessarily the date of a particular recording, reissue or compilation track); existing embedded/verified facts are not overwritten by the offline pass. Offline matching currently handles artist and release-group identities, **not** individual recordings without known albums or genres. Unmatched metadata may use existing online providers when enabled. Current matching covers queued local-library identities and retained HIVE listening history; it does not pre-index every unplayed track from all remote peer catalogues.\n\nFacts use the existing MusicBrainz reference representation and verified metadata feed. A separate snapshot/cursor keeps successful local metadata and avoids repeat lookups for unchanged identities. A new snapshot revisits unresolved jobs. Metadata caches are invalidated after factual matches so historical telemetry can be reclassified without replaying songs.\n\n## Validation and limitations\n\nRun `npm test`, `python3 tests/offline_index_test.py` and optional Chromium scripts. Full-size download and indexing, real Docker builds, Miniflare loopback networking and live Cloudflare deployments remain staging validation steps. No production state was touched. Do not delete Docker volumes to troubleshoot. If the service runs out of storage or is cancelled, the existing index remains installed; retry after resolving the problem.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-3-HIVE-METADATA-TELEMETRY-REFINEMENT.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.3 — HIVE Metadata & Telemetry Refinement\n\n- Telemetry opens on Week, with 7 rolling calendar days, 30 rolling days, 12 monthly buckets for the selected year and monthly history from the earliest retained play for All time. Empty buckets are preserved.\n- Nine telemetry tiles have independent time filters. Changing the top-level range resets tile overrides. Identical requests share cached results.\n- Docker scans stored tags and existing uploaded MP3 tags before online enrichment. Folder scans retain an atomic local index of file size and modification time on the existing state volume. Normal discovery still enumerates file names and stats; unchanged files are not parsed again. Full file rescan is opt-in.\n- Exact embedded facts can be shared with explicitly trusted admin peers. Consistent album release-year tags can classify missing historical plays; conflicting albums are not inferred. Embedded facts represent file tags, not independently verified release dates.\n- Docker optionally reads bounded MP3 tag byte ranges from trusted admin peers. Peers that do not support valid ranges are skipped; the whole song is not downloaded. Enable in Admin → Network → Telemetry, save settings, start scanner.\n- Separate local file preparation and online scan status, retry counters, provider-specific error names and cooldown countdown. Docker batch size supports up to 50 queued entities, not 50 simultaneous external requests. External provider pacing remains in force.\n- Cloudflare keeps bounded local work and supports incremental peer pulls. No volume mapping or original audio modification is required.\n\nFor an upgrade from v18.0.2, overlay the matching patch or use the full source. Back up databases and storage before deploying. Test Docker and Cloudflare separately; external service availability and live deployment are not guaranteed by local tests.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-2-HIVE-TELEMETRY-INTELLIGENCE.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.2 — HIVE Telemetry Intelligence (staging)\n\nBaseline: exact v18.0.1. The update is additive; existing music, R2 bindings, local database state, sessions, playlists, peer origins, metadata credentials and request history are not reset. No live deployment has been performed.\n\n## Docker-first enrichment\n\n1. Back up the repository, database state and music volume mappings. Verify adequate root disk free space before building.\n2. Deploy to a test Docker instance first. Confirm the version in the admin sidebar.\n3. Open **Admin → Network → Telemetry**. Save a valid MusicBrainz contact (email or URL), enable MusicBrainz and optionally Wikidata fallback. Keep frequently played priority enabled. Last.fm is optional and is not a release-year or artist-origin authority.\n4. Press **Scan missing metadata**. Docker performs persistent, bounded background batches independent of the browser. Pause, resume or stop at batch/job boundaries. The scanner rebuilds its work from retained history and library metadata; it does not re-download music. The progress bar is indeterminate while discovering jobs, then shows processed versus currently due jobs. A completed scan pass is not a guarantee every field was verified. Unresolved lookups remain eligible for later retry. Explicit Start after completion scans again; normal scheduled enrichment remains available for incremental follow-up.\n5. Enable network sharing and telemetry sharing on Docker (as appropriate for your installation). Ensure the Cloudflare Worker can actually reach the Docker peer using its registered endpoint (a private Tailscale address alone normally cannot be reached from a Worker). Configure appropriate secure connectivity; do not expose the internal scheduler port or scanner secret.\n6. On a staging Cloudflare WASP, register Docker as an administrator peer. In **Admin → Network → Telemetry**, enable trusted peer metadata, choose Docker as Preferred metadata peer, disable local scheduled enrichment if you want pull-only operation, and save. Click **Pull metadata from HIVE**. Peer-only pulls are incremental, up to 20 verified factual records per invocation; repeating or scheduling pulls moves the cursor forward. Configure a longer interval if D1 use is a concern. An unreachable peer reports an error; no fallback to remote providers is forced in pull-only mode. Only locally verified MusicBrainz/Wikidata facts are exported by default, no API keys or listening identities; imported facts are not re-exported as local validation. Last.fm sharing is subject to provider terms and off by default.\n\nCloudflare imports still use D1. Existing saved provider settings are respected; disable previously enabled local enrichment explicitly. Docker still uses provider limits, local disk, CPU and network. Unknown release years are never estimated. Provider 429/503 stops the continuous pass until the retry time; other unresolved fields are rescheduled rather than repeatedly hammered.\n\n## Telemetry accuracy\n\nA listen is confirmed at the existing 10-second playback threshold. A repeat cycle has its own play ID, while failover and transient delivery retries keep the same ID. Counter, recent history and telemetry ledger writes use a single database transaction. Browser retry queue persists in the current tab's session storage (bounded to 100 pending events / 24 hours). Delivery is best effort; closing the tab before retry or a permanent network failure can still lose a listen. Source relay remains best effort, while the listening instance records the confirmed event. The year filter now covers the requested whole year. The visible telemetry dashboard refreshes automatically (Docker 60 seconds, Cloudflare 5 minutes), retains the existing player and current country/decade selection, and has a manual refresh button. Cloudflare refresh may use cached statistics until expiry, to limit D1 reads. Coverage updates when verified facts are stored/imported.\n\n## Request synchronization and transient errors\n\nThe reported screenshots show a cross-request promise error and intermittent HTTP 500; there is no Worker stack trace proving that they have the same cause. This release prevents shared pending database initialization/cache promises between requests and retains only resolved values. HIVE request event/state/cursor persistence is transactional; a failed state write cannot advance the cursor. A bounded repair pass replays the existing ledger to restore missing request state, preserving newer completed/cancelled states and local dismissals. Admin queue retries a transient server error once and retains previously loaded rows if it still fails. Watch origin request completion, peer feed cursors, and the Worker error log during a three-server staging test.\n\n## Validation / rollback\n\nRun `npm test` under Node 22 or newer. Chromium UI scripts in `tests/` use mocked APIs and local browser media; they require Python Playwright and Chromium. Actual Docker image builds, provider connectivity and the Cloudflare Worker runtime were not validated here. Stage first; capture Worker logs on recurrence (do not share secrets). Do not delete persistent volumes or reset D1. For rollback restore the v18.0.1 source and rebuild against the same volumes/bindings after backup. Additive metadata/request settings may remain; a stopped scanner will not execute on the older source. Restore data snapshots only if a verified data fault warrants it.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-1-HIVE-REQUEST-PLAYLIST-RECOVERY.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.1 — HIVE Request & Playlist Recovery (staging)\n\nUpgrade baseline: v18.0.0. Back up the database, upload storage, configuration and Docker volumes. Do not delete inactive volumes. No change to the database bindings, mounted music folders, Caddy or existing routing and telemetry design. Schema changes are additive. This staging release was tested with Node/SQLite and Chromium fixtures, not deployed to a live service.\n\n## Admin → Requests\n\nSelect one or more requests and choose Refresh selected, or use Refresh on an individual row. Individual songs are completed only when the normalized recording title and artist match the local or eligible connected peer catalogue; a live recording is not substituted for a studio recording. Artist and album requests show distinct local and peer catalogue counts and require an explicit Mark as done. Counts deduplicate within each group; the same recording can appear in both counts. Completed items appear in Previous requests. Playlist requests retain the matching/missing-song progress display and stay active if incomplete. A mirrored request's local completion is not a cancellation or completion of the authoritative request on its origin. The origin may complete its own request when it observes a match. No cross-peer privileged write endpoint was added.\n\n## Origin tracking and housekeeping\n\nThe request feed identity must match a directly connected, authorized peer to identify its domain. Only administrators see the request-specific origin domain and can use Locate origin. A relayed request without a direct verified origin displays an unknown origin rather than falsely treating a relay as the origin. Network failures and server errors can mark an origin offline; disabled sharing, unverified identity and ambiguous HTTP responses are not treated as verified outages. An offline request shows Requestee is offline. Remove until online (also used for offline selected requests) hides it on this instance until a successful synchronization with that origin. It returns only if still active. Removal never affects audio or other peers. Ordinary v18 Remove / Remove all HIVE requests / Remove all requests retain their permanent local-dismissal semantics for remote requests, including offline requests in bulk clears; confirmation warns accordingly. Local cancellation remains federated. A remote-origin request is kept while incomplete unless the admin chooses to remove it.\n\n## Playlist recovery and Spotify import\n\nPrivate Spotify playlist imports support up to 1,500 known entries; previously an internal 100-entry cap truncated the available list. Spotify's public embed may expose fewer tracks than the actual playlist. The import displays matched, known missing, and (where a total is reported) titles not retrieved separately. Only known missing titles can be displayed. To supplement inaccessible Spotify titles, paste two tab-separated columns (title and artist) from a list you can access. Supplemental titles are user-supplied, not Spotify-verified. Spotify access is not bypassed.\n\nOpen Show songs not found in HIVE for per-song Search song and Request; search refreshes eligible peer snapshots in groups of four, up to 100 configured/approved candidates, and reports checked and responding peers. Catalogue matches do not guarantee that audio can be streamed. Automatic matching refresh uses bounded rotating peer subsets and cached catalogues. Unreachable peers may have stale or unavailable metadata. To finalize an incomplete private import, confirm Mark as done ANYWAYS; unmatched titles remain in the recovery list and Refresh matches can add them later. Existing deliberate track removals are preserved after partial finalization. An incomplete public admin playlist request is not forcibly completed by this private-owner action. Missing-song automatic request creation is bounded (up to four per draft run, ten per import owner per day, global active queue ceiling 500); individual Request buttons remain available subject to the same quota.\n\nPreviously unavailable playlist references display saved song, artist and album metadata when available. Search song searches eligible connected peer catalogues in batches, allows the owner to explicitly use a matching alternative, and changes only that playlist reference. If the old reference has no saved title/artist, enter them manually. Request registers the known song with normal request controls. No audio is deleted or moved. If a peer is temporarily offline or unreachable, search can only use available/cached metadata; playback itself is not verified by search.\n\n## Validation and rollout\n\nRun `npm test`, `python tests/ui_v18_0_1_refinements.py` and `python tests/ui_v18_features.py` (Chromium and Python Playwright required). Use the full ZIP for a clean source checkout or the changed-files ZIP only on exact v18.0.0. Stage Docker and Cloudflare separately; verify admin sidebar version `v18.0.1 · HIVE Request & Playlist Recovery`, confirm the database and volumes are retained, then check a long Spotify import, origin reconnect, and actual playback on the target network. Do not assume fixture tests verify Spotify service access, real D1 quotas or Docker build success. The v18.0.0 workerd Dockerfile fix is retained; allow adequate disk space for image rebuilds. No production deployment, credentials, or live request data included.\n"},{"name":"V18-0-0-HIVE-SMART-ROUTING.md","content":"# WASP v18.0.0 — HIVE Smart Routing\n\n**Staging release candidate.** Upgrade baseline: v17.0.7. This release does not deploy to any existing server automatically.\n\n## Native web player\n\nWASP retains alternate sources behind deduplicated catalogue rows. Candidates must match normalized artist, title, album and a duration within two seconds (or be the exact same source ID). This is a conservative metadata-based match, not a cryptographic guarantee of identical recordings. Unknown duration or album metadata prevents cross-source matching. The native player ranks up to six candidates using configured primary-peer preference, known offline state, observed playback startup latency, recent failures, anonymous active-stream counts, and a small randomized distribution factor. Primary preference is a weighting, not an exclusive pin. Demand observations are bounded to local and up to four peer endpoints, at most once per minute per browser; previous WASP versions without the routing summary remain playable. No audio is downloaded just to measure a server. This is browser-side routing, not an actual bandwidth measurement, a centralized load balancer, or a change to third-party Subsonic player behavior.\n\nOn audio errors or a ten-second stall, the same persistent audio element tries another eligible source through the normal peer relay, restores the last playback position once metadata is ready, and preserves the queue and logical track. Once all candidates have been tried, the final source gets one retry. If unavailable, the player stops with a status and a manual retry option. A source switch can produce an audible buffering gap; recovery depends on matching seekable audio and available peers. The confirmed-play threshold remains ten seconds into a track and a source switch does not double-count that track. No music is copied or deleted. Listener presence describes the source actually in use.\n\n## HIVE requests\n\nAdmin → Requests → Request queue provides individual Remove, Select visible / Clear selection, Remove selected, Remove all HIVE requests and Remove all requests. Every action asks for confirmation. Remote removals create persistent **local dismissals** by originating instance ID and request ID; they also hide the active request in this instance's public request display. A dismissed request continues to exist on its originating WASP and other peers; relaying and future completion history are unaffected. A newly submitted request with a different ID is not dismissed. Remove all applies to currently stored requests and is not a filter for future requests. Completed requests, uploaded music, playlists and accounts are not deleted. Removing a local active request cancels it on HIVE, unlike dismissing a remote request. Local bulk removal is bounded and continues in batches of eight. Migration creates a new dismissal table lazily and is additive; do not reset peer IDs or cursors.\n\n## Pull telemetry\n\nAdmin → Network → Telemetry → **Pull telemetry from peers** links to the existing Admin → Network → Crons → **Grab telemetry now** action. It reports imported event count, successfully read peer feeds, eligible peer count, errors and new request events. A run rotates through up to ten eligible configured or approved published peers; repeat if needed, or allow the existing scheduled sync to continue. Older/nonsharing peers may not provide telemetry. Default five-minute schedule and independent sharing controls are preserved. Requests may sync as part of the same job. The cached dashboard may take up to five minutes to reflect imported history.\n\n## Interactive listening charts\n\nMost listened → Telemetry: select a country on Artist Map to highlight it and show the top artist and that artist's most-played album for the current listening scope and date filter. Hover, focus, tap or click Music by Decade to highlight the decade and reveal its top album, album artist and artwork. The statistics are independent rankings of confirmed plays, not number of songs stored. Verified release-year/origin metadata drives classification; no invented origin or release year. Missing data displays a fallback. The WASP color theme and persistent audio player are preserved. Artwork resolution uses local art then known peer fallback. Both charts support keyboard selection.\n\n## Safe deployment and rollback\n\n1. Keep a copy of your exact v17.0.7 files. Back up the current D1 database and R2 or Docker SQLite state, uploads, music, and Caddy state. Test against a separate staging database and stack before promoting to production.\n2. Full ZIP contains the entire source. The changes-only ZIP may be applied **only to an exact v17.0.7 source tree**, preserving host-specific configuration and credentials. It contains paths relative to repository root. Keep the same Docker stack name, existing service/container names, volume mappings, Worker name and bindings for a genuine upgrade; those files are unchanged.\n3. Check EC2 free disk (`df -h`, `sudo docker system df`) before rebuilding. The Dockerfile now includes optional dependencies required by workerd and cleans the npm cache. Enlarging EBS and its filesystem may still be required; do not delete inactive volumes. The archive contains no npm lockfile because the original release did not contain one; dependency resolution remains dependent on the registry.\n4. Deploy to isolated staging first. Confirm admin sidebar label `v18.0.0 · HIVE Smart Routing`, existing accounts and playlists, local and peer audio seek, requests, telemetry, and reverse-proxy HTTPS. Stop one staging source during a song to test real failover. Then try a genuine single-source failure and manual retry, and verify no double-count. Check D1 quota/read usage under representative traffic.\n5. To roll back, redeploy exact prior source against a consistent backed-up state. The additive dismissals table is ignored by older versions, so dismissed requests may reappear after rollback. Never run `docker compose down -v` on your live stack. Do not replace a production D1/R2 binding with a disposable one by accident.\n\n## Validation limits\n\nNode tests use a SQLite-backed D1 test adapter. Chromium tests use fixture APIs and real browser media with synthetic WAV sources (failure/resume are exercised without remote servers). They do not exercise real network transport, live Cloudflare D1 quotas or third-party mobile clients. This environment has no Docker CLI and cannot resolve the npm registry, so image build and live Wrangler/Worker deployment could not be validated. This is **not a production-certified release**. See the accompanying test report for regression results and outstanding baseline failures.\n"},{"name":"V17-0-7-HIVE-METADATA-COVERAGE.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.7 — HIVE Metadata Coverage Enhancement\n\nStaging release from exactly v17.0.6. This update improves historical listening metadata and adds the requested coverage monitor at **Admin → Network → Telemetry**. It does not change music playback, network routing, user data, or music requests. Smart routing remains reserved for v18.\n\n## Coverage monitor\n\nThe monitor shows all retained local and imported HIVE listening events, not just the current public dashboard period. It uses the same release-year and country precedence as the public charts. Release-year coverage counts each play once; country coverage counts each play once. The percentages can differ from a filtered public dashboard. An empty ledger shows a dash rather than claiming full coverage. Counts are cached for up to five minutes and invalidated after successful metadata batches. If the coverage query fails, the settings remain accessible and the panel displays an unavailable state.\n\nThe **Scan missing metadata** button runs one bounded manual batch using saved settings. Its result is displayed below the button. For unattended scanning, enable scheduled metadata enrichment (five minutes by default). Save settings before scanning; manual runs respect the scheduler's cooldown/lease. The prioritized setting ranks jobs by the number of discovered listening events and favors artist/album jobs on ties. The counts reflect discovered portions of retained history; the scheduler walks the ledger in 30-event chunks, so priorities improve as history is discovered. This does not replay any listening events. Enrichment continues through the local track index after the history cursor reaches its end.\n\n## Provider behavior\n\n- Keep Last.fm and MusicBrainz settings from v17.0.6. Last.fm is used for tags; it does not provide reliable album release-year or artist-origin fields. Set a valid MusicBrainz contact email or HTTP(S) URL, enable MusicBrainz, and allow fallback when Last.fm is preferred.\n- MusicBrainz first-release-date for a matching release group is saved once per normalized artist and album. It takes precedence over recording-level date, then recorded event/embedded track metadata. This lets matching songs on the same album reuse one lookup. A named album is not assigned a new recording-first-release-year simply because a release-group lookup fails. Older recording-level fields remain as fallback. An unknown album can still use recording-level metadata.\n- Artist geography comes from MusicBrainz's begin-area country code where present, then its artist country/associated area. An associated country is a proxy for origin if more precise formation/birth data is not available. Wikidata is an optional enabled-by-default fallback within the existing fallback switch, using exact-name entity search and structured album publication date, country of origin, formation location or birthplace claims, not IP address or listener location. Only unique matching structured results with a single usable year/country are accepted. Wikidata also requires the saved valid administrator contact. Ambiguous names, guest credits, various-artists compilations, international groups, disputed dates, missing API records, unknown languages and unavailable provider responses may remain unclassified.\n- Provider order still respects the saved preference and fallback switches. If Last.fm is the only enabled provider or fallback is disabled with Last.fm preferred, the factual fields will remain limited to information already available locally or from trusted peers. Last.fm-derived redistribution remains opt-in with permission; no credentials are exported.\n- Trusted administrator-connected peers running this release can exchange verified album-level and Wikidata factual records over the existing metadata feed. Origin and listening-event cursors are preserved. Older WASP versions can keep exchanging normal telemetry but do not understand album-level records or Wikidata records. Imported peer metadata is not re-exported as locally verified. User-published peers are not implicitly trusted. The trusted-peer option is not cryptographic provider authentication.\n\n## Bounded processing\n\nA default batch checks one entity. Settings allow up to 3 entities for installations with headroom; when trusted peer imports are enabled, local work is capped at one entity per batch to reserve SQL budget. A batch stops starting new work after approximately 20 seconds, and each upstream fetch has an 8-second timeout. A maximum of 3 peer metadata records are imported per cycle, with cursor advancement restricted to processed entries. Discovery uses atomic bounded multi-row writes (at most 80 bind parameters per statement). Intra-batch calls to the same provider are spaced by at least 1.1 seconds; provider rate-limit responses defer the job and avoid additional calls to that provider in the same batch. This is not a global rate limiter across different servers or separately initiated tests. External lookups never execute on ordinary listener page loads. Only the cached coverage and an occasional SQL aggregate are used for monitoring. Actual Cloudflare CPU/rows-read usage depends on the real data and must be verified in staging; use 1 entity per batch on Free Workers.\n\n## Deployment\n\n1. Back up D1 or the Docker persistent state and the existing v17.0.6 source; apply the changes-only patch to exactly v17.0.6 or deploy the full source. Preserve all environment variables, secrets, mounts and peer settings.\n2. Deploy the Cloudflare **test** Worker first or a staging Docker instance. Confirm v17.0.7 in the admin sidebar. The metadata schema migration adds `play_priority` to `hive_metadata_jobs` and uses new discovery/coverage cache keys. Existing telemetry, metadata, credentials and cursors are preserved. No destructive migration is performed.\n3. Open Admin → Network → Telemetry. Confirm saved provider configuration, enable MusicBrainz and Wikidata fallback as appropriate, and test MusicBrainz. Enable scheduled enrichment and save. Choose **Scan missing metadata** to request a bounded batch. Check coverage over subsequent cycles, not just immediately after deploy. A provider miss or rate limit can cause no progress in a particular batch.\n4. Verify public decade and artist-origin charts, and that audio continues when navigating between pages. Check Cloudflare invocation logs for CPU/memory/D1 errors. Do not reset telemetry or peer cursors.\n\nRollback: redeploy the previous full v17.0.6 source if needed; the new schema column and cache keys are additive. Do not roll back by deleting tables. v17.0.6 cannot use the new album-level cache correctly for charts; the event ledger stays intact.\n\n## Tests\n\nLocal Node SQLite and mocked provider/browser tests cover album release group matching, history-wide reuse, popular music priority, coverage, migration, peer sharing, ambiguous results, Wikidata data handling, credential handling, and playback navigation. Provider responses and 2,252-play preview values are test fixtures. No live Last.fm/MusicBrainz/Wikidata key or actual Cloudflare/Docker deployment was tested. Real data completeness is not guaranteed.\n\nReferences: [MusicBrainz release-group search](https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API/Search/ReleaseGroupSearch), [artist search](https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API/Search/ArtistSearch), [Wikidata Action API](https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/API:Presenting_Wikidata_knowledge), [Wikidata music properties](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:WikiProject_Music).\n"},{"name":"V17-0-6-METADATA-INTEGRATION-HOTFIX.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.6 — HIVE Metadata Integration Hotfix\n\nStaging update from exactly v17.0.5. No schema changes, reset, music movement or playlist migration is required. The persistent audio player and existing telemetry event identities remain unchanged.\n\n## Settings improvements\n\nAdmin → Network → Telemetry now has stacked provider sections, themed native controls, separate connection messages and responsive fields. The Last.fm and MusicBrainz tests can use current entered values before saving. A successful test does not save credentials. Save settings separately, then test again to verify saved configuration. Blank secret fields preserve previously stored values. Failed saves preserve entered fields so they can be retried. The interface reports HTTP status for HTML/non-JSON errors without rendering upstream HTML. An indeterminate progress bar represents a running bounded batch, then reports the actual result; it is not a percentage of the entire library. Counters show unique track/album identities and artists with verified fields, and total verified fields. Missing provider results remain unknown.\n\n## Cloudflare setup\n\n1. Under the test Worker's Settings → Variables and Secrets, use a persistent `METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY` secret of at least 24 characters. Generate a unique value with `openssl rand -hex 32`; never paste real keys into Git or screenshots. Existing encryption keys must remain available until previously encrypted credentials have been migrated or explicitly replaced. Do not casually change a Spotify encryption key.\n2. `METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY` is an encryption key, NOT a Last.fm API key. Enter the real Last.fm API key in WASP and click Save settings, or configure `LASTFM_API_KEY` as a Worker secret. `LASTFM_SHARED_SECRET` is optional for read-only calls. Saved credentials take precedence over runtime values.\n3. Choose and enable the preferred provider. MusicBrainz needs a real administrator contact email or URL, not an API key. You may disable MusicBrainz if you cannot supply a contact, but Last.fm alone does not reliably provide release years and artist origins.\n4. Test the selected provider, save and test the saved configuration, then enable scheduled enrichment or run a batch manually. An HTML HTTP 503 response is not evidence of an invalid Last.fm key; inspect Worker invocation logs for the underlying failure (including Error 1102).\n\n## Docker / Portainer setup\n\nSet optional `METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY`, `LASTFM_API_KEY`, and `LASTFM_SHARED_SECRET` in the private stack configuration and rebuild/redeploy without deleting persistent volumes. The startup script now creates a private, mode-0600 local Wrangler `.dev.vars` file containing those supplied bindings, without copying credentials into the image or command-line arguments. Existing scanner-secret fallback remains available. The existing Spotify encryption-key binding behavior is intentionally unchanged to avoid breaking old credentials. Existing Spotify credentials are never reset by this release. Treat the app container and its private runtime filesystem as sensitive.\n\n## Credential compatibility\n\nThe metadata credential reader attempts the configured dedicated key followed by the previous Spotify/scanner fallback keys where available. Saving a replacement encrypts with the current preferred key. If no available key decrypts older saved data, the interface asks for explicit replacement; no automatic erase occurs. Preserve old installation secrets securely until the new credentials are saved and tested. Rotate any exposed credentials carefully; use distinct keys per purpose and installation.\n\n## Validation and limitations\n\nThe backend tests exercise provider settings, encryption/key compatibility, error responses, and generation of private local binding files. Browser tests use mocked API endpoints and assert responsive layout, credential save/test/retry, batch status and playback continuity. Live Last.fm/MusicBrainz API calls, Cloudflare Workers resource use, Docker rebuild and private-peer connectivity remain to be tested with the actual staging deployments. The existing small batch budget and metadata sharing permission defaults are unchanged. Smart Routing and automatic failover remain reserved for v18.\n"},{"name":"V17-0-5-HIVE-METADATA-RESOURCE-OPTIMIZATION.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.5 — HIVE Metadata Intelligence & Resource Optimization\n\nStaging release from the exact v17.0.4 baseline. The persistent player, stream routing, audio objects, accounts, playlist ownership, telemetry event IDs, peer cursors, Spotify completion rules and existing configuration are retained. HIVE Smart Routing / adaptive load balancing / playback failover remain planned for v18.\n\n## Set up metadata enrichment\n\nOpen **Admin → Network → Telemetry** (`/admin#network/telemetry`).\n\n1. Choose **MusicBrainz** or **Last.fm** as the preferred provider. MusicBrainz is the initial preference. Enable the desired providers and fallback to the other enabled provider for fields not yet verified.\n2. MusicBrainz public noncommercial lookups are keyless. Enter a real administrator contact email or URL for the application's identifying User-Agent. Review the service's usage terms and rate limits. Test the connection after saving configuration.\n3. For Last.fm, put the API key in the masked field. The shared secret is optional (not required for read-only track and artist lookups). Saving credentials through the admin UI requires an installation encryption secret: `METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY` (at least 24 characters), or the existing `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` / Docker `SCANNER_SECRET`. On Cloudflare, add the installation key via Worker Settings → Variables and Secrets (secret type), or `npx wrangler secret put METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY`. Use a strong random value and preserve it through deploys; replacing or losing it prevents decryption. Docker Compose exposes the optional `METADATA_SETTINGS_KEY`, `LASTFM_API_KEY`, and `LASTFM_SHARED_SECRET` environment variables. Set them via secrets or your private stack configuration, never in Git. Cloudflare can use environment secrets `LASTFM_API_KEY` and `LASTFM_SHARED_SECRET` too. A saved credential overrides the runtime environment values. The backend uses AES-GCM to store admin-supplied credentials and never returns them to a client or peer.\n4. Enable scheduled metadata enrichment (opt-in), save settings, test, and click **Run batch now** if desired. Five minutes is the default interval. Intervals are 5, 10, 15, 30, 60, and 120 minutes. Manual runs have a cooldown to avoid stampeding; the status reports the most recent bounded batch. Ordinary telemetry sharing remains independently controlled in Network → Crons.\n5. Keep Last.fm redistribution off unless the administrator has the necessary permission. Enable **Trust verified metadata from administrator-connected peers** only for a registry whose administrators you trust. Approved user-added peers still supply listening telemetry; their field claims are not automatically trusted for the new verified cache. If Last.fm redistribution is permitted and enabled on both servers, eligible Last.fm genre fields can also be reused.\n\n## Metadata architecture\n\nThe new `hive_metadata`, `hive_metadata_jobs`, `hive_metadata_cursors` tables are additive. Historical and newly imported plays are discovered in sequence order, in small pages; the local library is also discovered incrementally after the historical event backlog. Remote-only library tracks enter this verification queue when their listening events arrive. Jobs are keyed by artist and exact track/album identity (artist-only jobs collect country/tag facts). Each automatic or manual batch attempts at most one entity and at most one call per enabled metadata provider, not one call per listen or every song in a playlist. Failed fields are retried later; verified nonempty fields are reused, not replaced simply because provider preference changes. Initial enrichment takes many cycles; empty/ambiguous results remain unknown.\n\nMusicBrainz supplies a recording's first-release year and its artist's associated country/area code, where an unambiguous exact search match exists. It can also supply tags. Artist area is a provider-reported association, not listener location or an independently verified birthplace. Last.fm supplies track/artist tags with identity checks; wiki publication timestamps are not release dates, and Last.fm does not reliably provide dedicated country/release-year fields. Fallback is useful for those fields. The cache records provider, reference URL, timestamp and whether verified on this host or reused from a trusted peer. Only a small subset of response data is retained; no full upstream payloads are cached. Last.fm fields have a conservative installation-level insertion budget (10,000 new fields; no claim of measuring all provider-specific bytes across an entire network). Site operators must comply with the actual API agreements, including attribution, caching, and commercial-use conditions.\n\nMusicBrainz factual release-year and country fields can be exchanged via `/api/telemetry/metadata/feed` separately from the ordinary `/api/telemetry/feed`. The sharing endpoint sends a cursor-limited page of 20 locally verified fields with provenance, subject to existing library/telemetry sharing settings. MusicBrainz supplementary tags are not exported by this feed. Last.fm fields are not exported unless the administrator explicitly enables permitted redistribution. Trust is administrative, not cryptographic proof of an upstream response. The receiver checks a directly configured trusted administrator peer in rotation, skips fields already known and never re-exports peer-supplied facts as locally verified. Thus this version does not implement transitive proof/federation across arbitrary peers. Losing access to a peer is not a reason to erase a library or listening ledger. The new feed has its own cursor; historical enrichments do not replay listening events or change a play's UUID. Older WASP versions remain stream/catalogue compatible but cannot exchange this new provenance feed. Cloudflare cannot reach private Tailscale endpoints without a reachable route.\n\nThe existing telemetry summaries join the new cache to listening records, including peer and old historical events whose metadata was missing. Music by Decade includes segmented rows from Pre-1960 through the current decade, hover values and a highlighted top album where known. Unknown years are excluded and coverage remains visible. Origin heat map uses provider-reported country, not the location of the listener. Artwork first attempts the local endpoint; on failure the browser tries up to three registered, reachable peer image endpoints, prioritizing the origin when known and using Docker's same-origin `/_peer/<id>` relay when supplied. This is bounded browser fallback, not a persistent server-side artwork mirror. Offline peers and missing art can still produce a placeholder.\n\n## Request queue resource optimization\n\n`GET /api/admin/requests` now reads saved request availability snapshots (counts, missing songs and check time), rather than opening and parsing the entire local and peer library once per requested playlist. The database removes the redundant original Spotify track list from the queue response. Legacy requests with no snapshot initially say availability check pending. Use the existing Refresh button for a single request or allow the rotating scheduled matcher to update it. The queue can be stale; completion still checks availability and enforces v17.0.4 verified/unverified source rules before marking done. Individual refresh/completion and the bounded background matcher still use the stored catalogues and can be resource-intensive on exceptionally large libraries.\n\nMatching uses temporary normalized-title maps rather than repeated whole-library searches for every song. Count-only checks avoid generating peer reference hashes unnecessarily. Periodic metadata enrichment, request reconciliation, private Spotify draft refresh, and peer catalogue refresh run in separate minute slots within each five-minute cycle. The automatic request matcher processes one pending request per cycle; drafts and catalogue refresh are similarly bounded. Uploads no longer rescan every requested playlist synchronously. Very large queues take longer to converge. This targets the observed Cloudflare HTTP 503 / 1102 queue-read path; real Worker CPU, memory, and D1 row-read budgets have not been measured, and this is not a guarantee that every operation fits Cloudflare Free limits.\n\n## Tests and deployment\n\n- `node --test tests/*.test.mjs` includes mock Last.fm and MusicBrainz identity matching, encrypted credential storage, historical aggregation, trusted peer reuse and sharing controls, cache-backed request queue and completion verification.\n- `python tests/ui_v17_0_5_metadata.py` checks the new admin subtab with mocked endpoints; `python tests/ui_v17_0_5_peer_art.py` verifies fallback URL selection in the sandbox (not actual peer image delivery); `python tests/ui_v17_0_1_charts.py` exercises the segmented chart and existing interactions; `python tests/ui_v17_playback_continuity.py`, `python tests/ui_v17_0_2_hive_controls.py` and `python tests/ui_v17_0_4_spotify_completion.py` cover regression behavior. Tests do not use real Last.fm keys, public providers, real Cloudflare D1/R2, Docker stacks, or actual production peers.\n- Apply the changes-only patch to exactly v17.0.4, or use the full source archive. Back up persistent D1/R2/SQLite data, environment values, existing source, and Docker volumes first. Do not run destructive migrations or `docker compose down -v`. Deploy the test Worker first; confirm the admin version, load Requests, refresh one playlist and check completion. Then configure providers, confirm a small batch runs, and observe Worker CPU metrics and the telemetry coverage over multiple cycles. Preserve the old deployment for rollback; additive metadata tables may remain safely when rolling back code.\n\n## Provider references and future APIs\n\n- Last.fm API: https://www.last.fm/api ; terms: https://www.last.fm/api/tos\n- MusicBrainz API: https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API ; rate limits: https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API/Rate_Limiting ; data licence: https://musicbrainz.org/doc/About/Data_License\n- Future provider candidates (not integrated in this release): https://soundcharts.com/en/blog/music-data-api\n"},{"name":"V17-0-4-SPOTIFY-REQUEST-COMPLETION.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.4 — Spotify Request Completion Fix\n\n## Problem addressed\n\nThe test Cloudflare Worker returned HTTP 409 for `POST /api/admin/requests/{id}/complete` for a Spotify playlist showing `50/50+ · 50 local · 0 missing`. v17.0.3 sets `partial` whenever an embed exposes **exactly 50** or **100 or more** tracks, and rejects any partial request without giving the administrator a safe way to finalize only the visible subset. The admin UI also mislabels some completion errors as `Couldn't refresh.`\n\n## Behavior in v17.0.4\n\n- Extract optional **playlist-level** track count from the public Spotify embed's playlist entity when present. Do not guess a total from unrelated nested objects or infer an exact length merely from receiving 50 tracks. When the reported total equals the retrieved list length, classify the list as **verified**; when it exceeds the retrieved list, classify as **known incomplete**.\n- Keep compatible behavior for existing shorter public-embed lists when no exact source total is available. Such lists are *not* advertised as cryptographically or independently verified.\n- For an unverified boundary-sized list (including existing stored v17.0.3 requests with only `partial=true`), when **all retrieved songs are locally or remotely available**, display **Finalize N retrieved songs…**. A separate admin confirmation states that the full Spotify playlist length is unknown and additional Spotify songs will not be included. The completion API requires `{ \"confirm_unverified\": true }`. The acknowledgement, retrieval count and timestamp are stored in the request's existing JSON metadata for auditability. The completed request moves to Previous requests.\n- A known-incomplete playlist (e.g., Spotify reports 70 but its embed provides only 50) **cannot** be finalized even with the override. Likewise, a request with any missing retrieved songs cannot be finalized. The request stays in the active queue, with a helpful explanation.\n- The admin interface displays the API's specific `error` message, or a fallback with HTTP status, for both Refresh and Completion. It no longer calls completion failures `Couldn't refresh.`\n- Admin-created playlists keep their matched tracks in Spotify's retrieved order, accept local/peer references as before, and retain the existing Spotify image fallback. No music is uploaded, deleted or copied as part of this fix.\n\n## Known limits\n\nSpotify's public embed is not an authoritative complete-song API. Spotify might omit songs or change its shape at any time. An *unverified* completion intentionally creates a playlist **only from the retrieved tracks**, even when additional songs exist on Spotify. Previously imported private user Spotify playlists are not changed by this admin request fix. For lists of fewer than 50 visible tracks with no reported total, v17 preserves existing completion behavior even though the overall Spotify total isn't independently verifiable.\n\nIf the public embed cannot provide a complete source count, obtaining a truly verified playlist may require a future authorized Spotify integration or user-supplied list. Do not claim that a manually finalized subset represents every song in the original Spotify playlist.\n\n## Upgrade and rollback\n\n1. Back up your D1 or Docker persistent database state. Make a Git commit or backup of exact **v17.0.3**.\n2. Apply the **v17.0.3 → v17.0.4 changes-only patch** to that exact baseline, or use the full v17.0.4 source on a separate staging branch. Keep Docker music, DB, settings and peer volumes mounted as before.\n3. Deploy test Worker and open **Admin → Requests → Request queue**. Open a 50/50+ completed-visible-song request. Verify that the new **Finalize N retrieved songs…** button and explicit warning appear. Confirm, and verify it appears in Admin Playlists and Previous requests.\n4. Confirm that requests with missing songs stay pending and that a playlist with a known higher source count does not offer the override.\n5. Verify peer playback and ordinary audio navigation still work. No player source, streaming endpoint or peer sync was modified in this patch.\n\nNo SQL migrations or media transformations are needed. Rollback is a code redeploy of v17.0.3; previously completed playlists and requests remain stored.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- `node --test tests/*.test.mjs`\n- `python tests/ui_v17_0_4_spotify_completion.py`\n- `python tests/ui_v17_playback_continuity.py`\n\nThe local tests use mocked Spotify responses and a local sqlite-like test database, not your live Cloudflare Spotify playlist. Production behavior still requires a real staging verification.\n"},{"name":"V17-0-3-HIVE-PEER-SYNC-HOTFIX.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.3 — HIVE Peer Sync Redirect Hotfix\n\n## Root cause confirmed on Docker\n\nThe user's Docker D1 `telemetry_peer_cursors` table showed **both bridge peers stalled at cursor 0** with:\n\n```text\nInvalid redirect value, must be one of \"follow\" or \"manual\"\n```\n\nThe same Docker container could retrieve `/api/peer` and `/api/telemetry/feed` successfully through Tailscale / Nginx using `curl`. The underlying problem was the unsupported Fetch API option `redirect: 'error'` in WASP v17.0.2, not the bridge, endpoint permissions or peer registry.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Use supported `redirect: 'manual'` for telemetry `/api/peer` and `/api/telemetry/feed` pulls, and federated HIVE request feeds.\n- Explicitly reject all HTTP 3xx, opaque redirect responses and already-followed responses rather than following a redirect to an unapproved origin. Upstream 403/404 remain legacy/non-sharing peer skips.\n- Apply the same safety-preserving correction to connected-peer catalogue revision checks for Spotify imports, approved Spotify playlist cover-image retrieval and the Mobile Bridge's optional peer biography fallback.\n- Report build v17.0.3 in Admin and update asset cache-busting versions. No schema migrations, event conversions, cursor resets, new media fetches or audio-player changes.\n- Add regression tests using a mock compatible with Wrangler's supported redirect modes, including rejected redirects and persisted error messages.\n\n## Install and verify\n\n1. Back up Docker's persistent state and any production D1 database before upgrading, and preserve credentials, volumes, peer URLs and Tailscale settings.\n2. Apply this changes-only patch **only to exact v17.0.2** or deploy the full v17.0.3 project on a staging branch. Rebuild the Docker image; a container restart without rebuilding an old image will not install this fix.\n3. Admin → Network → Crons → **Telemetry grab → Run now**. Confirm the operation reports at least one peer checked and ideally imported events. Source peers must have network/telemetry sharing enabled. Re-run if there are more than 200 outstanding events per peer.\n4. Check Docker's locally persisted D1 cursor state using the existing Wrangler D1 config, e.g. `SELECT url,cursor,synced_at,last_error FROM telemetry_peer_cursors;`. Previously failed peers should advance beyond `0` and clear `last_error` when their feeds return valid events.\n5. Check `/api/telemetry?period=year&scope=hive` for `connectedCaches`, `lastSynced`, `nodes` and `plays`. Aggregate JSON may be cached up to **five minutes**. Different years/time windows can hide otherwise imported data.\n6. Test federated request pulls, peer playback, Mobile Bridge and browser audio continuity. The hotfix does not touch browser player logic.\n\n## Operational caveats\n\n- **Only incoming sync is fixed** for the private Docker-to-public Cloudflare path. A public Cloudflare Worker still cannot directly pull an unexposed Tailscale/private Docker service. A public, securely reachable route or an intermediary capable of propagating origin-tagged events is needed for reverse sync.\n- Missing genre/artist country tags and missing peer ranking artwork involve separate metadata and art-routing limitations; this hotfix does not manufacture metadata or change artwork resolution.\n- An HTTP redirect from any peer is deliberately rejected; fix the peer's registered base URL or Nginx upstream so `/api/peer`, `/api/telemetry/feed` and `/api/hive/requests/feed` return a direct HTTP 200, without redirects.\n- Do **not** delete peers, clear `telemetry_peer_cursors`, drop D1 tables, wipe Cloudflare state, or delete Docker volumes to apply this update.\n- Safe rollback: deploy the prior v17.0.2 image/commit; no schema change requires reversal. However the old redirect bug will recur until the hotfix is restored.\n- Passing simulated tests does not prove a live Cloudflare Worker or AWS container deployment. Run the above real-peer verification before promoting to production.\n"},{"name":"V17-0-2-HIVE-REQUESTS-SPOTIFY.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.2 — HIVE Requests, cron controls, source indicators and private Spotify recreation\n\n## What changes\n\n- `Admin → Network → Crons` has a **Run now** control and persisted execution status/progress for every maintenance job. The Docker SHA-256 scanner continues using its own read-only scan progress.\n- **HIVE Telemetry** is the only newly enabled default maintenance cron: every **5 minutes**, with configurable 5/10/15/30/60/120-minute intervals. Unchecking **Share my telemetry with the HIVE** stops this server's public telemetry feed; **incoming telemetry is still pulled** by the scheduler/manual operation. Turning sharing off does not erase copies previously synchronized to other peers.\n- Songs and artists display compact **local server**, **peer cloud**, or **both** source icons. Audio playback and peer music sources are unchanged.\n- Public song/artist/album/Spotify-playlist requests are shared across approved administrator-managed peers and approved online member-published peers as origin-tagged, deduplicated *read-only* request records. New requests can be discovered after the next synchronization cycle. Requests are not removed when one server receives a peer event. The originating server retains final ownership of completion or cancellation.\n- Spotify playlist requests include separate **local** and **peer** already-present track counts, based on saved verified peer catalogue snapshots rather than matching the playlist title alone. A playlist remains pending until explicitly completed; a partial match does not close it.\n- Signed-in members can paste a **public Spotify playlist link under My Playlists → Private playlists**. The system creates a **private** playlist with the available Spotify cover image, inserts exact local/approved-peer matches in original order, and posts **only missing individual songs** into the federated request queue. The owner's *Refresh matches* checks for newly available tracks; *Mark as done* is owner-only and available only when all **known** songs match.\n- Missing Spotify admin configuration variables are documented for both Docker/Portainer (`SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY`, `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID`, `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET`) and Cloudflare runtime secrets. No real secret values are bundled.\n\n## Important limitations\n\n**Spotify's public playlist embed is not a reliable, complete library API.** The importer can process only the tracks Spotify makes available through the public embed; the embed may omit a portion of a larger playlist or change at any time. The importer refuses an empty song list, marks obvious 50-/100-track truncation as partial, and prevents final completion while known tracks are missing or truncation was detected. If the embed exposes a truncated list without an advertised count, WASP cannot infer omitted tracks. Imported music is referenced, **never downloaded or duplicated**. The source playlist cover is fetched only from approved Spotify image hosts.\n\nFederated requests use the existing opt-in public network-sharing boundary, not an end-to-end authenticated account protocol. A malicious participating server may fabricate its **own** request or telemetry records; operators should approve peers they trust. The feed strips visitor tokens, user IDs, session credentials and private playlist data. Each receiving server treats remote requests as **read-only**. Music fulfilment depends on actual catalogue metadata and a reachable playable source, not merely on a peer stating “done.”\n\n**Scheduled synchronization is eventual, not instantaneous.** Each run visits up to 10 peers (rotating through larger HIVE networks). Only suitable reachable v17.0.2 peers exchange request records; older v15/v16 peers remain music compatible but do not implement the new request feed. The host behind a private Tailscale bridge may not be reachable directly from Cloudflare. For multi-hop request sharing, each intermediate peer must run v17.0.2 and opt into public network sharing.\n\nCloudflare's `waitUntil` work has finite lifetime. If a manually started job is interrupted, the stored status reports an error after a timeout; retry the job and inspect the Worker logs. Long operations such as metadata checks process bounded batches rather than locking the web player.\n\n## Deploy on staging first\n\n1. Back up the staging D1 database or Docker state volume, user playlist data, and the current Git commit. Do **not** delete Docker volumes or overwrite production credentials.\n2. Apply **only** the upgrade patch to the **exact v17.0.1 baseline** or use the full v17.0.2 source for a new staging installation.\n3. In Cloudflare use runtime secrets, never build variables. On Docker define `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` in the private Portainer environment (or retain the existing Docker `SCANNER_SECRET` fallback). Do not change an already-used encryption key or lose access to saved Spotify credentials.\n4. Verify **Admin → Network → Crons**: only telemetry is enabled by default on a clean install, interval 5 minutes. Run a manual telemetry pull, check progress and last errors, and verify source sharing can be disabled without preventing a pull.\n5. Connect two or more v17.0.2 staging servers and an approved member-published peer. Submit a request on one server and verify it appears on another, with the same origin/request ID and no duplicate after a relay loop.\n6. Import a short public Spotify playlist as a signed-in member. Check private visibility, cover, exact local+peer matches, individual missing-song requests, automatic matching on later catalogue updates, and explicit owner completion.\n7. Test source badges on local-only, peer-only and duplicated tracks; confirm peer playback, HTTP Range, Mobile Bridge and normal playlist playback still work during navigation.\n\nRollback: return to the previous Git commit and Worker build / Docker image. Schema changes are **additive** and should not be destructively rolled back. Restore the backed-up database only if data must also revert; don't revert or delete music volumes. Newly imported member playlists remain in the database unless explicitly removed by their owner.\n"},{"name":"V17-0-1-TELEMETRY-VISUAL-REFINEMENT.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.1 — HIVE Telemetry Visual Refinement\n\n## Summary\n\nIncremental staging update **from exactly v17.0.0**. No changes to the browser's persistent `<audio>` player, P2P audio path, Mobile Bridge routes, account settings, credentials, user playlists, or existing player behavior.\n\n- Listening clock: a 24-segment radial graphic with hour-level tooltips, strongest-hour summary, keyboard-focus labels, and the viewer's reported timezone offset.\n- Listening history: exact date/bucket and confirmed-play count on hover/focus; the existing period-specific bucketing remains.\n- Top tags: multi-genre, layered trend bands with labeled counts, hover/focus highlighting, and per-tag peak data when available. These are *tagged plays*, not a partition of total plays; a track can carry several genres.\n- Music by decade: release-decade distribution with accurate count and percentage of all confirmed plays, artist/album/track counts, and top recorded album/artist for each decade.\n- Artist map: lightweight, locally served world geometry and graduated country heat layer. Hover/focus reports counts and artist diversity. Countries are displayed only if verified ISO3 artist-origin metadata exists. No inference from listener IP or browser locale.\n- All visual work remains confined to lazy-loaded `public/telemetry.html`, `telemetry-view.js`, and `telemetry.css`. All chart interactions are browser-native; nothing is sent to a third-party analytics or advertising service.\n\n## Metadata collection and transparency\n\nThe existing v17 event ledger gains **additive** `genre`, `release_year`, and `country_code` fields. v17.0.1 also creates small local metadata tables for verified ID3 release years, genres, and artist-origin codes. Older event rows are preserved. Album/artist/track identities in the event ledger are not recalculated or overwritten.\n\n- Existing artist Wikipedia/Wikidata genre tags (`artist_info.genres`) can support the genre chart immediately. Local track-level ID3 `TCON` genre tags can also contribute from new Cloudflare uploads, and Docker file scans provide ID3 genre values.\n- Docker scanner reads `music-metadata` `common.year` and `common.genre` for new/indexed files. Browser MP3 uploads can supply `TYER`, `TDRC`, and `TCON` tags. Existing Docker files are re-read on scanner startup; already stored Cloudflare tracks that lack release-year tags in the database cannot be historically reconstructed without reliable metadata re-ingestion.\n- The world visualization needs **verified origin country** codes. These fields can be carried between authorized v17.0.1 peers when known; the system intentionally does **not** invent a country from an artist's name, IP address, or genre. A server with no origin metadata shows the map with neutral countries and explains the missing coverage.\n- Genre/decade/country groups use local SQL aggregates cached per period and scope. The telemetry feed remains origin-aware and deduplicated, strips source track IDs from peer-facing events, and never includes user IDs, mobile credentials or private session metadata.\n- Before data is available for a period, charts say so instead of displaying fabricated statistics. The sample previews supplied with the release are explicitly illustrative.\n\n## Deploy and roll back\n\n1. Back up the staging D1 database (or Docker persistent state) and record the working v17.0.0 Git revision.\n2. Apply the changes-only patch over exactly v17.0.0, or use the full source to build a separate staging deployment. Never use a changes-only patch on v15/v16.\n3. Preserve `MUSIC`, `DB`, environment variables and Docker volumes. **Never run** `docker compose down -v`.\n4. Confirm the Admin sidebar version reads **17.0.1**. Open `/#top/telemetry`, check the radial clock, date history tooltips, genre highlights, heatmap and decades. Empty metadata must produce honest empty states.\n5. Play a track, navigate Telemetry / Hearts / Likes / Dislikes / Albums and back. Audio source, playback state and position must not be reset.\n6. Sync two compatible peers and confirm that one event contributes once, with its existing source identity preserved. HTTP Range streaming and mobile sessions must still function.\n\nThis locally tested ZIP is a staging candidate, not a production migration guarantee. Real Android, Cloudflare, Docker, live peer connectivity and dataset-specific metadata must be verified before production rollout. The source packaging doesn't deploy to any live site.\n\nWorld boundaries are based on Natural Earth public-domain low-resolution country geometry, bundled locally (no external map requests).\n"},{"name":"V17-0-0-HIVE-TELEMETRY.md","content":"# WASP v17.0.0 — HIVE Telemetry & Loading Time Enhancement (staging release)\n\n## Purpose\n\nWASP v17 keeps the existing browser-based WASP player, P2P protocol, Cloudflare Worker, Docker stack, playlists, and optional Subsonic Mobile Bridge while introducing an **independently loaded HIVE Telemetry feature**. The chart is **website-only**; this release adds no Subsonic analytics UI or mobile-app requirements.\n\n## Navigation and playback guarantees\n\n- The music player and its `<audio>` element remain in `public/index.html` **outside** the `#list` content region. WASP never re-instantiates that element when navigating between `/#top`, `/#top/telemetry`, `/#hearts`, `/#likes`, `/#dislikes`, and other ordinary views.\n- The first telemetry visit loads `public/telemetry.html`, `telemetry.css`, and `telemetry-view.js` through a same-origin iframe within the content area. Switching between the telemetry and reaction tabs preserves the already loaded iframe. Leaving the feature releases its chart resources. Neither case reloads the main document or audio source.\n- The sidebar routes are unchanged. `/#top` shows the **Most listened / Telemetry** subtab bar. `/#top/telemetry` includes selectable **week/month/year/all-time** periods, years and months, previous/next period, and **Entire HIVE / This server** filters.\n- Most listened, Most hearted, Most liked, and Most disliked use the lazy-loaded feature document and WASP's existing catalogued playback, votes, and peer library metadata. Clicking a song sends a same-origin, source-validated message to the parent to use the **same player**.\n- **My Playlists stays a normal page and Account Settings stays a popup**, because bundling those functions into a new HTML file does not by itself improve load time. The account dialog and normal playlist views continue to load their data as needed.\n- The admin **Insert sample slider** now inserts seven first-party **Featured Slides**, with slide 7 **ONE HIVE. EVERY BEAT.** after slide 6 **Shuffle the pit**. Existing customized slides are preserved. The first-party sample has no third-party ad/tracking requests. The new shuffle message type is `wasp:featured-shuffle`; old saved carousels using `ad:shuffle` remain supported.\n\n## Confirmed-listen ledger and data correctness\n\nThe backend adds *only* these tables/indexes (through a resumable, additive migration):\n\n- `telemetry_events`: source-owned unique event ID, origin server ID, UTC time, song title, artist, album, duration metadata; `UNIQUE(origin_id,event_id)` prevents repeat imports and sync loops.\n- `telemetry_peer_cursors`: per-peer replication progress, node ID, last sync/error.\n- `telemetry_summary_cache`: short-lived indexed historical summaries by period and scope.\n- `settings` entries for instance identity, migration marker and bounded sync leases.\n\nA confirmed WASP browser listening event is written to this ledger without disrupting the old Recently Listened flow. Successful *completed* Subsonic scrobbles also contribute; simple stream downloads or buffering are **not** treated as confirmed plays. For a v17-to-v17 proxied peer play, the relay signals the audio source to avoid adding a second ledger event. `recent_listens` is retained unchanged and provides **up to 1,000** previously retained entries as an initial, explicitly incomplete backfill. v15/v16 instances without the v17 ledger cannot supply their lost historical entries.\n\nStatistics provided: confirmed plays and comparison totals, unique artists/albums/tracks, top artists/albums/tracks, monthly/daily charts, weekday and hourly charts, participating origin-server count, activity streaks, busiest day/hour, and anonymized aggregate listening-fingerprint indicators. The dashboard **does not invent** music-genre tags, artist countries, year-of-release charts, exact personal listening hours, complete old history, or user leaderboards when trustworthy data is absent. Source song duration is *not* proof of total listening time.\n\n## Federated P2P telemetry\n\n- On **every participating v17 server**, the `GET /api/telemetry/feed` API exposes confirmed anonymous music events **only while that server's existing library/network-sharing setting is enabled**. Event IDs are stable, real user IDs / usernames / tokens / session IDs / IPs are not transmitted, original precise timestamps are rounded to **five-minute buckets**, and source-server identities are preserved through relay hops.\n- A receiving server reads the **existing admin peers plus approved and online user-published peers**, verifies their public `/api/peer` sharing response, imports a bounded incremental feed, and keeps a cursor per peer. At most 200 entries per peer request; up to 10 peers per sync cycle with rotating selection so no approved peer is permanently excluded when there are more than ten.\n- Replication is **origin-aware**, not a sum of peer totals. The same event relayed via A→B→C and later C→A is deduplicated. Synced events do **not** carry mobile keys or personally identifiable account data. Private playlists and listening identities remain on their original server.\n- Cloudflare runs this job from its existing Worker scheduled handler (normally every five minutes). Docker calls the same backend sync from its existing private cron poller, via `ctx.waitUntil`, so slow peer synchronization cannot delay the HTTP cron response. A database lease reduces duplicate refresh work. `POST /api/admin/telemetry/sync` provides an authenticated, explicit sync for an admin, if needed.\n- Every server keeps its own local copy of the imported ledger and cached summaries. A disconnected peer does not stop music playback or make already-cached charts disappear; a \"last synchronized\" timestamp makes staleness visible.\n\n### Privacy, trust and federation limitations\n\n- **Network sharing must be explicitly enabled on each source server**. Telemetry is anonymous **aggregated public HIVE data**, not a server-wide transfer of personal listening history; private per-user history and opt-in user leaderboards are **not implemented** in this release.\n- Trusted peers and submitted links must be deliberately configured/approved. Source node IDs are locally generated and not cryptographically signed in this version. A malicious or compromised peer can fabricate its own telemetry. Therefore cross-server totals are *best effort*, not audited listening proof. Future signed-peer identities, opt-in personal rankings, and robust origin revocation need separate security design.\n- Confirmed-play events are written without sending full listening-session data between servers. Legacy v15/v16 peers continue to provide music and playlists but cannot yet participate in telemetry exchange; they are skipped without breaking peer connectivity.\n- The public telemetry feed exposes title/artist/album listening trends to anyone who can access a shared server; operators should only enable network sharing for music they are comfortable making publicly visible. Do not reuse it for sensitive listening metadata.\n- Historical coverage starts at v17 plus whatever limited, retained Recent Listen events survive migration. Long-term large-HIVE deployments need storage/daily-rollup monitoring and further retention policy work. Cloudflare Free D1 row and storage quotas may constrain sync volume; dashboard queries use 5-minute caches rather than re-aggregating per render. Remote peer time buckets can slightly affect edge-of-period counts.\n\n## Deployment and rollback\n\n1. **Staging first.** Apply the v17 upgrade patch **only to exactly v16.0.6**, or use the complete v17 staging package in an isolated repo. Take a verified D1/SQLite backup, preserve R2 and Docker volumes, keep the preceding Git commit, and avoid changing production Cloudflare Worker bindings.\n2. Deploy the backend and static frontend together. Run `npm run build:docs` (or the usual `npm run deploy`) when building manually. Keep the existing Worker/staging name and Docker bindings and secrets so updates use the same persistent data. Never delete the state volumes and never run `docker compose down -v`.\n3. Confirm the admin sidebar version reads `17.0.0`. Check `/#top/telemetry` through the **same WASP hostname** and switch `/#top`, `/#hearts`, `/#likes`, `/#dislikes` while real audio is playing. Check the audio element, track source, playhead and pause state are unchanged.\n4. Add/verify a mutually connected pair of isolated v17 installations, including a Docker instance if possible, with `network_share` explicitly enabled. Listen to a known local song, relay a peer song, and verify exactly **one** event per actual confirmed listen across peers after synchronization. Test reconnection, multi-hop A→B→C→A and no extra counts after duplicate feeds.\n5. Test period navigation and stale views: month/year/week, local versus HIVE, old year with no data, user-approved versus unapproved peers, offline and outdated peers. Ensure the app still serves music when telemetry storage/query fails.\n6. To roll back, restore the previous code commit/build and deployment bindings. **The additive v17 telemetry tables will remain** and should not be manually deleted as a shortcut; full database restore requires a verified snapshot. Previous code does not depend on the new tables.\n\n## Validation performed in development\n\n- Node backend suite and WASP regressions, dedicated feed/caching/multi-peer deduplication tests.\n- Headless Chromium checks that an actively playing audio track, original audio element and media source remain unchanged while switching Most listened, Telemetry, Hearts, Likes, Dislikes and Albums.\n- Standalone telemetry UI smoke at desktop/mobile sizes: charts, filters, changing period/scope, responsive layout.\n- Existing browser smoke tests for collapsed sidebar, Account Settings popup, Mobile Bridge credentials, and Currently Streaming.\n\n**The above are local tests.** Real Cloudflare/Docker peer federation, production D1 usage, real-world media playback and multi-device responsiveness must be verified in staging before promoting the upgrade to a live installation. This release does **not** modify a running user server automatically.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-6-MOBILE-FAVORITES-ALBUMS.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.6 — Album and Subsonic compatibility hotfix\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Soundtracks/compilations on `/#recent/albums` are grouped by normalized album title rather than the track performer's exact name. Generic titles remain artist-scoped; album pages open the full grouped record. This is a metadata heuristic, not MusicBrainz release matching. Albums with identical distinctive names but genuinely different recordings can still merge; this release does not delete or modify music files.\n2. Desktop sidebar retains scrollability but hides the native scrollbar during expand/collapse. Border divider remains visible; narrow/mobile layout is unchanged.\n3. Mobile `star`/`unstar` store account hearts locally in both `user_song_reactions` and the same `reactions` account voter identity the web player uses. Peer stars use stable, origin-specific WASP reaction IDs and never mutate the source peer. Browser guests have their own anonymous reactions; signing in allows account-scoped hearts to synchronize. Account deletion cleans up account-voter reactions.\n4. `getStarred`, `getStarred2`, song `starred`, `getArtist`, `getArtists`, search artist metadata, `getCoverArt` for artist IDs, and `getArtistInfo(2)` now expose compatible data. Artist biography requests trigger native cached Wikipedia lookup when needed; peer-only artists may fall back to a trusted source's public `/api/bio`. Peer requests do not receive mobile passwords or cookies. If sources have no biography, empty results remain possible.\n5. Admin Advertising → **Insert sample slider** now provides the agreed six-slide HIVE example (join the swarm at slide 5, shuffle at slide 6). Existing saved custom ads are unaffected.\n6. Playlist-list artwork and its Android-client-specific behavior are out of scope, per request.\n\n## Safe validation\n\n- Apply the patch to **v16.0.5 only**, preferably on staging. Check `/api/admin/version` for `16.0.6`.\n- On `/#recent/albums`, confirm the same multi-artist compilation appears once and that opening its tile lists songs by multiple performers. Check a generic `Greatest Hits` remains artist-separated.\n- Collapse and expand the desktop sidebar, including with tall credits. Check no native scrollbar flashes and scrolling still works.\n- Sign into the same WASP account in the browser and Android. Heart and unheart both a local and peer-only track, refresh both clients, and confirm changes synchronize with the same account. Different accounts and logged-out guests should remain independent. The legacy browser's cached state may take one polling cycle to update.\n- Search an artist in Android, verify artwork via `getCoverArt` and `artistImageUrl`, then open bio. Test a peer-only artist; unavailability should fall back gracefully. Confirm no mobile credential is forwarded to peers.\n- Do not reset Mobile Bridge secrets or remove existing Docker volumes. Do not assume a v16 staging release is approved for v15 direct replacement.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The database historically stored account hearts without their original timestamps. Older hearts therefore use an explicit epoch fallback in Subsonic `starred` metadata; this is a compatibility marker, not an assertion of when the user pressed heart.\n- Some mobile clients ignore optional artist-image fields or cache responses. Refresh the client when testing. Other Android client-specific features, including playlist-list thumbnails, depend on the particular app implementation.\n- This hotfix is locally tested; the user's actual live Docker/Cloudflare peer network and Android app have not been accessed or modified by the patch.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-5-MOBILE-LIVE-PRESENCE.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.5 — Subsonic mobile presence (STAGING HOTFIX)\n\n## Summary\n\nThis targeted update addresses the missing **Currently Streaming** status for music played through third-party Subsonic/OpenSubsonic Android clients. WASP's native browser player already sent anonymous play/pause heartbeats; the Mobile Bridge previously accepted Subsonic playback and completed scrobbles but never registered those sessions in the live-status table.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Successful authenticated `GET /mobile/rest/stream.view` (or `/rest/stream.view`) requests record one anonymous active session per **issued mobile app credential**, for both local and approved peer tracks. Existing stream proxying, Range responses and Web playback are unchanged.\n- `scrobble` with `submission=false` (playback start) also records the presence. It **does not** increment completed listen counts; `submission=true` retains the existing confirmed-scrobble behavior.\n- Each credential has a stable opaque session identifier. Seeking and subsequent byte-range requests for the same track don't inflate the listener count. When the client switches songs, the same credential's live entry moves to the new track.\n- `download` and `HEAD` requests are never counted as playback; a failed peer stream isn't counted either. No account name, device ID, credential or listener IP is exposed through `/api/active-streams`.\n- Entries use WASP's existing `active_streams` table and public summary endpoint. No schema migration or additional media storage is necessary. Native WASP browser heartbeats retain their current 65-second expiry.\n\n## Limitation: approximate third-party streaming state\n\nUnlike the WASP HTML audio element, third-party Subsonic clients do not reliably send pause, stop, or periodic heartbeat events. To keep short songs and normally buffered tracks visible, a Mobile Bridge session expires **15 seconds after the reported track duration**, bounded between **65 seconds and 6 minutes** (or 2 minutes when duration is unknown). Additional valid stream requests can renew it. This is a best-effort estimate, **not proof that sound is playing continuously**. Pausing, bulk prebuffering and offline cached playback may cause false positives or missed listening. Only sessions on servers whose `active-streams` endpoints are reachable as connected peers can appear in other servers' cross-peer dashboards.\n\n## Deployment and verification\n\n1. Back up your separate v16 installation's D1/R2 or Docker state and pin the current version. Apply this hotfix on top of **v16.0.4**, or deploy the complete v16.0.5 staging package.\n2. Rebuild/redeploy the backend and static assets. Keep existing mobile credentials, Mobile Bridge encryption secret, network peers and music volumes. **Do not reset the database**.\n3. Confirm `/api/admin/version` shows v16.0.5. In Admin → Network, leave the Mobile Bridge enabled, and use an existing valid device key in the Android Subsonic client.\n4. Play a track from the **same WASP server's Mobile Bridge** and open `/#listened` in another browser. The streaming icon and listener count should show the song after the next normal refresh (up to ~10 seconds). Try a local song and an approved peer-only song.\n5. For a dashboard on *another* WASP installation to show this activity, that dashboard must have the mobile-hosting WASP server registered as a reachable peer; activity isn't automatically broadcast to unrelated servers. Check `GET /api/active-streams?summary=1` on the mobile-hosting server first.\n6. Pause and stop tests: a Subsonic client with no stop event may remain visible until the bounded expiry; this is an expected compatibility limitation, not an accurate continuous presence heartbeat. Verify the web player still stops its status promptly when paused.\n7. Confirm `getPlaylist`, `getCoverArt`, `/audio`, and peer streaming continue to work as before. The hotfix does not modify play count, music files, playlists or federation state.\n\nThis release is locally tested, but real-device live presence still requires verification on the target Subsonic client. Do not promote over an older v15 production installation without a separate migration rehearsal.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-4-PLAYLIST-ARTWORK.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.4 — Playlist artwork compatibility hotfix (STAGING)\n\n## Why this update exists\n\nThe Android Subsonic music client can connect to WASP v16 and display server and user playlists with their song entries, but v16.0.3 omitted the `coverArt` identifier from the Subsonic playlist response. The client therefore showed a generic music-note tile. This update corrects the API response and supplies the requested image bytes through `getCoverArt`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `getPlaylists` and `getPlaylist` emit an opaque, stable `coverArt: pl:<playlist-id>` for every playlist visible to the signed-in account. No source URL, device secret, or private peer address is included in the metadata.\n- `/mobile/rest/getCoverArt.view?id=pl:<playlist-id>` (and `/rest/getCoverArt`) checks the same user authorization as the playlist listing. Other members' private playlists and their covers cannot be fetched.\n- Server-managed playlist images are fetched from the existing R2 object `art/playlist/<id>` when a custom cover is set. Member playlist images use `art/user-playlist/<id>` when a standalone cover is selected.\n- Synchronized public peer playlist covers are fetched on demand via the **approved source's** existing `/art/playlist` or `/art/user-playlist` endpoint. No mobile key, session cookie, or Authorization header is sent upstream. Redirects are not followed and arbitrary cover URLs are not used as upstream hosts.\n- If a playlist has no standalone image, its first accessible track's normal WASP track art is used as a fallback. That may be a track thumbnail rather than the webapp's JavaScript-generated nine-tile mosaic.\n- OpenSubsonic `readonly: true` is reported for playlists because this staging compatibility layer supports read-only playlist access.\n- All changes are additive to the API layer; no music, playlist rows, peer snapshots or existing mobile keys are altered. No new database migration is required.\n\n## Installation\n\n1. Apply the v16.0.4 artwork hotfix over **exact v16.0.3** in the separate staging repository, or deploy the v16.0.4 full staging package.\n2. Rebuild and deploy the Worker/static assets. Keep the same D1, R2 and persistent Mobile Bridge secret; do not reset the secret or regenerate device keys.\n3. Open Admin → Network → Connected Peers; synchronize the trusted peer sources if previously unsynchronized. This fix does not itself re-sync the catalogue.\n4. In the Android client, refresh the server playlist library and clear cached playlist images if needed.\n5. Verify a server playlist with uploaded art, a member playlist with uploaded art, a public peer playlist whose source exposes art, and a playlist without custom art. Check `getPlaylists`, `getPlaylist`, and authenticated `getCoverArt` responses. Test a peer-only song for correct playback and seeking separately.\n\n## Verification and limitations\n\nNode regression tests cover `coverArt` metadata, fetched PNG/JPEG bytes, no-art fallback, private-playlist isolation and credential stripping on peer artwork requests, plus existing mobile/P2P functionality. These tests do not establish universal compatibility with every Subsonic client. The uploaded Android screenshots verify successful connection and playlist entries **before** this artwork fix, not that artwork has been verified on-device afterward. A few clients only refresh thumbnails when their local image cache is cleared.\n\nThis is still a staging release. Do not replace the current v15 production Worker without a separate production-migration rehearsal and backups.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-3-MOBILE-PLAYLISTS-FOLDERS-KEYS.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.3 — Mobile Playlist, Folders & Short Keys (STAGING)\n\nThis is a staging update on top of **v16.0.2**. It does not alter the WASP-native player or peer protocol and must be verified with real Android clients and the production v15 peer network before any production migration. No MP3 is copied between peers.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Playlist sources:** OpenSubsonic `getPlaylists` and `getPlaylist` now expose: WASP-managed server playlists (`playlists`/`playlist_tracks`), the signed-in user's own playlists, other members' explicitly public playlists, and synchronized public server/member playlists from permitted peers. Private playlists belonging to others are never listed. Server playlist IDs use a new `sv:` prefix to distinguish them from member playlists and remote IDs.\n- **Usable song counts and entries:** Song counts are calculated from resolved, currently accessible WASP tracks instead of being hard-coded as zero. Borrowed songs in member and server playlists are resolved through `peer_track_refs` to authorized origin + remote track ID. Remote playlists resolve `track_sources` when available. Unavailable, unshared and untrusted tracks are not claimed playable.\n- **Music folders:** OpenSubsonic `getMusicFolders` exposes a virtual combined WASP library, local music, and source-specific folders for peers whose music is available in the synchronized catalogue. `getMusicDirectory` can browse source → artist → album → track; `getIndexes`, `getArtists`, search, and album list accept `musicFolderId`. These are virtual groupings, not real filesystem directories. **Some Android apps require Navidrome-specific behavior for their Folders UI**, which cannot be guaranteed by standard Subsonic endpoints alone.\n- **8-character codes:** New device credentials use cryptographically random A–Z and 0–9 characters, exactly eight characters. Preexisting 16-character or older longer credentials remain valid and revocable. The button reveals the code once in a centered bold pill, with a copy icon and clipboard-success message.\n- **Security:** An 8-character base36 code provides approximately **41.4 bits of entropy**, notably less than the previous 16-character credentials. For this staging design, the server persists and enforces 12 failed authentication attempts per 15-minute window, scoped by username (or client network address for key-only authentication). Authentication throttling does not make short codes equivalent to long, high-entropy tokens. Use HTTPS and revoke exposed device keys. WASP-managed server encryption is unchanged; no secret needs to be reconfigured.\n\n## Supported P2P scope\n\nThe mobile adapter uses the same existing v16 federation snapshots as v16.0.2: music on the v16 installation, direct configured/synced WASP peers, and public HTTPS downstream sources explicitly registered by an approved v15 gateway (one hop). Private network addresses, unshared sources, and disconnected or unsynchronized sources cannot be discovered automatically. Peer audio continues to be fetched from the allowed source's `/audio/{id}` using byte-range streaming; browser cookies and mobile keys are **never forwarded to peers**.\n\n## Upgrade test process\n\n1. Back up v16 staging D1/R2 and pin your currently deployed v16.0.2 Git commit. Do not apply this incremental patch to v15.0.9 or the live `KNDPZ/WASP` installation.\n2. Apply **v16.0.3 upgrade patch to the existing v16.0.2 staging repo** (or deploy the complete v16.0.3 package in a new isolated test environment). Rebuild/redeploy both the Worker and static assets. Preserve existing bindings and storage.\n3. Verify Admin version **16.0.3**, Mobile Bridge enabled, encryption ready, and the existing per-device keys still work. Do not reset the master secret.\n4. Under Admin → Network → Connected Peers, re-sync your approved v15 gateway and check the Tracks / Playlists / Users / Federation sync results. Remember that peer snapshots, not the bridge UI itself, determine which downstream music is available.\n5. On Android, reconnect or refresh the library. Verify `getPlaylists` contains server, member, and peer playlists; open them to check playable entries, counts and search. Try a track available only on a peer, seek within it, and verify the source does not receive device credentials. Test Folders and note any app-specific Navidrome limitation.\n6. Generate a fresh 8-character key in Account Settings → Mobile, test the centered copy button and confirmation, and verify that an old key still works until revoked. Rotate any previously exposed individual mobile key (without resetting the server-wide secret).\n7. Validate the native WASP web player, P2P connections and public Request page have not changed. Test rollback by redeploying your pinned v16.0.2 commit while preserving databases/volumes.\n\n## Known limitations and production gate\n\n- Real Android Subsonic clients vary in their support for nonstandard virtual folder trees. In particular, the user's screenshot shows a client saying its Folders view requires Navidrome; this release cannot guarantee it will enable that client-specific feature.\n- Folders cannot expose an origin if that origin is not trusted, not synchronized, or has no accessible tracks. Not every public peer necessarily provides all playlist/track metadata.\n- **Do not promote to production solely from automated tests.** First validate with your actual v15 gateway, two or more peer-only tracks, at least one server, member, and peer playlist, mobile seek/range support, authentication rate limits and backup/rollback plan.\n- Code and migration changes are additive. The Mobile Bridge uses a new table `mobile_auth_limits` for authentication throttling; existing music and playlists are not rewritten.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-2-MOBILE-FEDERATION.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.2 — Federated Subsonic Mobile Bridge (STAGING)\n\nThis release fixes v16.0.1's mobile catalogue limitation: local music and direct synchronized peers worked, but public music and playlists **behind** an existing WASP v15 gateway were not automatically included in compatible Subsonic/OpenSubsonic apps. This update remains **a staging candidate** until verified with your actual connected v15 sources and Android client.\n\n## What changes\n\n- **WASP stays native.** This is an adapter inside WASP. Original `/api/*`, web player, settings, peer protocol and uploads stay authoritative; no new Subsonic server or duplicate MP3 library is created.\n- Mobile Bridge now reads local songs, direct synchronized v15 music and **one level of public HTTPS peers registered by that v15 gateway**.\n- Peer media requests go to the real source's `/audio/<id>` with byte ranges and without cookies, mobile secrets or admin credentials. No audio files are copied. Redirects are rejected; if a duplicate is available from more than one peer, WASP can retry another trusted source.\n- Public peer playlists and publicly shared member playlists from those downstream sources appear in `getPlaylists`. `track_sources` mappings resolve cross-peer playlist entries, including the borrowed remote tracks in public member playlists.\n- `getArtists`, `getAlbumList2`, search, album and track metadata now see the federated catalogue. `getGenres` / `getSongsByGenre` work **when a source has real genre metadata** (from tags or public `/api/genres`). We do not fabricate genres for tracks with no classification.\n- New keys are **16 mixed-case alphanumeric characters**, cryptographically generated using rejection sampling. Previously issued long keys stay valid and revocable. Only use app-specific keys, never WASP website passwords.\n- The admin peer Sync button refreshes federation metadata when Mobile Bridge is enabled. Automatic peer-catalogue sync crons also refresh federation. The optional second-hop feature does not run when Mobile Bridge is disabled.\n- Additional D1 tables `mobile_federation_sources` and `mobile_federation_chunks` store **metadata only**. They are created additively. No existing music or playlists are deleted or modified.\n\n## Recommended upgrade procedure (your current setup)\n\n1. **Back up your v16 staging D1 database and R2 state**. Keep the v15 production deployment untouched. Do not push staging code to the v15 production Git repository. Save the current v16.0.1 Git commit as your rollback point.\n2. Extract the v16.0.2 upgrade patch into your existing **v16 staging repository** (`wasp-test`) and push. Do not overwrite or delete existing staging bindings.\n3. Confirm Cloudflare deployed the new staging commit, and **Admin → version** reports `16.0.2`.\n4. Under **Admin → Network → Mobile Bridge**, check that its status is configured/enabled. You can retain your current WASP-managed server secret and existing mobile device keys. **Do not reset the server secret.**\n5. Under **Admin → Network → Connected Peers**, make sure `https://wasp.playy.online` (v15 gateway) is configured and its music is shared. Click **Sync** for this peer. Let `Tracks`, `Playlists`, `Users` and the final federation stage complete. The completion message now displays extra downstream peer/song/playlist counts and any source warnings.\n6. If v15 has downstream peers, check that they use publicly reachable **HTTPS** URLs. A source accessible only via Tailscale, a private IP, a hostname blocked by the network, or HTTP-only URL will not be auto-discovered for Internet/mobile use. In that case, add an authorized public HTTPS source directly to v16 or expose it through an approved public endpoint. Do not weaken production-network controls.\n7. In an Android Subsonic client, use `https://YOUR-STAGING-HOST/mobile` (or the base host if the client ignores subpaths), your v16 member username, and the WASP Mobile key. Refresh the client library; some apps cache music collections locally.\n8. Confirm **Artists**, **Albums**, **Search**, **Playlists**, **Genres** (where metadata exists), playback of a remote-only track, seeking/Range (`206`) and completion scrobbling. Compare mobile search results against the music **actually available from the v15 network**, not only v16 local tracks.\n\n## How discovery works and the trust boundary\n\nOnly the admin can register a v15 gateway in v16's Connected Peers. On authorized synchronization, the staging Worker asks the gateway's **existing public read-only** `/api/network/peers` and `/api/peer-catalogue` APIs for the downstream list and previously synchronized metadata. It also confirms that the gateway and each downstream source actively report an explicitly shared library via `/api/peer`. It excludes user-submitted community entries from automatic URL discovery and ignores private IPs, `localhost`, private-style domains, HTTP URLs, nonstandard ports, URL credentials and non-root paths. Only one downstream level is traversed (no uncontrolled recursion). If an approved source is missing from the cached gateway snapshot, the Worker probes that source's public `/api/peer` endpoint and reads its read-only `/api/tracks`, `/api/playlists`, `/api/user-playlists` and `/api/genres` APIs.\n\n**Limits:** Up to 15 downstream sources per gateway; 6,000 tracks and 500 playlists of each kind per source; size-limited JSON and D1 metadata chunks. These are intentionally conservative staging limits. Larger networks require pagination or incremental synchronization rather than increasing limits recklessly. A source that becomes inaccessible can leave stale metadata visible until the next valid sync, but media playback cannot succeed while the source is unavailable. Peer URLs remain on the server; mobile API song identifiers do not expose the source URL. Only sources with a reachable audio endpoint can actually stream.\n\nTo keep the current system stable, a failure in federated discovery **does not roll back the direct peer snapshot** and does not affect the webapp's own catalogue. Warnings are reported in the admin Sync status.\n\n## Backend tests and known limitations\n\nRun `node --test tests/*.test.mjs` (includes the original v15 regressions and v16 federation tests). These tests use mock peers, sqlite fixtures and mock HTTP streams and do not touch your actual v15 accounts or music.\n\nKnown limitations:\n\n- Some Subsonic clients implement optional endpoints differently. The actual Android app must be retested before promoting v16 to production.\n- Genre browsing can still be empty if tracks and artists have no genre tags/metadata. WASP cannot infer a real genre reliably from a song title alone.\n- Peer access requires a stable, public HTTPS endpoint. The v16 Worker does not bypass private networks or expose private bridge endpoints.\n- Playlist **editing**, mobile live-playback heartbeat detection and transcoding are not implemented. Read-only playlist browsing, searching and original-audio streaming are the present scope.\n- Some source playlists may reference audio from an origin no longer approved by the gateway or absent from its synchronized snapshot; those entries are intentionally omitted rather than streaming from an arbitrary URL.\n- Cloudflare Worker network egress, origin latency and bandwidth can affect playback. No production performance promise is made from local tests.\n- A v16.0.1 rollback does not understand the new federation tables (they can remain harmlessly in D1), but mobile federation disappears until this release is redeployed. Your existing short and long mobile credentials remain valid with the same managed secret.\n\n## Production promotion gate\n\n**Do not deploy directly into the live v15 Worker.** First verify your separate `wasp-test` Worker with actual v15-backed music. If successful, take a production backup, preserve the original `KNDPZ/WASP` release commit and deployment configuration, and prepare a controlled upgrade of the production code with separate feature enablement/rollback. Do not use `docker compose down -v` or destroy D1/R2 resources. This ZIP does not perform any deployment automatically.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-1-MOBILE-BRIDGE-MANAGED-SECRET.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.1 — Mobile Bridge Setup & Security (STAGING)\n\n**This release is intended for the separate v16 staging repository only.** Do not overwrite the live v15 Cloudflare Worker or Docker database. Existing v16.0.0 staging installations can upgrade in place **without dropping their D1 database, R2 bucket or Docker volumes**.\n\n## What changed\n\n- Admin → **Network** now contains three subtabs in order: **Connected peers / Mobile Bridge / Crons**. The old Mobile Bridge card is removed from General settings.\n- Admin → Network → **Mobile Bridge** shows distinct operational states: enabled/disabled and encryption configured/missing. It shows the current mobile server address.\n- A new **Generate secure secret** button creates a random 256-bit server key and saves it under the staging D1 `settings` table. No Cloudflare runtime `MOBILE_BRIDGE_SECRET` is required for a **new installation**.\n- **Use existing secret** accepts a securely generated 64–128-character hexadecimal string. Do not use hashes of dictionary words, ordinary passwords or a shell command as the secret value.\n- **Move existing secret into WASP** safely migrates a pre-existing v16.0.0 runtime secret into managed storage without changing the effective encryption key or revoking device credentials. Do this **before** removing a legacy Cloudflare Worker secret, if you already issued keys.\n- **Reset server secret** requires the exact word `RESET`; it generates a new random secret and atomically deletes all existing mobile app keys/scrobble deduplication receipts. **All mobile clients must generate new app keys afterward.** Music, playlists, users and their listening history are not deleted.\n- Account Settings → Mobile clearly indicates setup is required; its Create Key button is disabled and visually muted. The backend rejects key generation as well when no secret is configured.\n- Native WASP webapp, original WASP API, v15 peers, music storage and public Request are unchanged.\n\n## Deployment: Cloudflare staging\n\n1. Make a D1 database backup. Check that your `wasp-test` Worker is bound to **staging-only** D1 (`DB`) and R2 (`MUSIC`).\n2. Update **only** the staging Git repository with v16.0.1, then redeploy its Worker. Keep your production v15 Worker unchanged. Do not create a new D1 or overwrite existing storage.\n3. Sign in as the staging administrator. Go to **Admin → Network → Mobile Bridge** (deep link: `/admin.html#network/mobile`).\n4. If status says **Setup required**, click **Generate secure secret**. Wait for **Encryption configured**. You can leave Mobile Bridge disabled until then. No Cloudflare runtime secret setup or GitHub build variable is necessary.\n5. Check **Enable Mobile Bridge on this server**, then **Save Mobile Bridge settings**. Confirm the status reads enabled and ready.\n6. Sign in with a regular staging member account; Account Settings → Mobile → **Generate mobile app key**. A unique app secret appears only once. Use that in the Subsonic app with `https://YOUR-STAGING-HOST/mobile` (or the root host for clients that disregard paths). Never use the website login password in a mobile app.\n7. Verify `https://YOUR-STAGING-HOST/mobile/rest/getOpenSubsonicExtensions.view?f=json`, then test authenticated `ping.view`, streaming and seek/range. Monitor Worker logs but don't expose URL query tokens.\n\nIf Cloudflare reports a legacy runtime secret, click **Move existing secret into WASP** while the old environment secret is still available. Once the UI indicates the source is WASP-managed, you may remove the now-unneeded legacy runtime variable using Cloudflare Settings → Variables and Secrets. **Do not remove an existing secret before migration** if you want to retain compatibility with previously generated device keys.\n\n## Deployment: Docker / Portainer staging\n\nExisting Docker installations auto-generate a persistent `SCANNER_SECRET` for unrelated scanner functions. Mobile Bridge can reuse it for backwards compatibility or migrate it to WASP-managed D1 with the admin button. You do not need to change Docker credentials or volumes to get Mobile Bridge running. Retain the app state volume during redeploy and back it up. This update does **not** remove Docker's `SCANNER_SECRET`, which is still used by the folder scanner.\n\n## Security model and limitations\n\n**Trade-off:** WASP-managed setup is more convenient, but storing the server encryption key and legacy encrypted app-password material inside the **same database** weakens separation against a database-read compromise. D1 encrypts at rest but does not provide the same isolation as a Cloudflare runtime secret. Restrict D1 and admin access, retain backups, enable MFA on host accounts, and keep staging separate from production. Never expose the settings row through a public API, logs or screenshots.\n\nThe server secret protects the **encrypted legacy Subsonic app-password representation**. Each device also has a one-way SHA-256 key lookup hash; mobile credentials are shown once and revocable. API credentials can be placed into HTTP query strings by some clients, so use HTTPS, don't log them, and don't send them to untrusted peers. Native WASP passwords and session cookies remain separate. The older Cloudflare runtime secret is supported **only as a compatibility/migration fallback**; new installations do not depend on it.\n\nResetting the server secret immediately invalidates all Mobile Bridge device keys. The secret value is never returned in admin status responses or the member settings API. An administrator must be authenticated to generate/import/migrate/reset the secret; member accounts cannot access these actions.\n\n## Rollback\n\nThe new secret is stored as one additional D1 `settings` row: `mobile_bridge_secret_v1`. No existing music tables or files are modified. Rolling back code to v16.0.0 does **not** make that older version understand the database-managed secret; mobile credential generation could stop unless a suitable runtime secret is restored. Keep the v16.0.1 build available for rollback of unrelated frontend changes, or disable Mobile Bridge without reverting the rest of WASP. A backup of the staging D1 database is recommended before setup or reset.\n\n## Testing\n\n`node --test tests/*.test.mjs` validates the Mobile Bridge lifecycle and WASP backend regressions. `python tests/ui_v16_mobile_bridge.py` checks member settings. New tests additionally cover missing-secret rejection, setup, import, migration, reset/revocation and UI/navigation. Live mobile client compatibility is still an **on-device staging validation requirement**.\n"},{"name":"V16-0-0-MOBILE-BRIDGE-STAGING.md","content":"# WASP v16.0.0 — Mobile Bridge (ORIGINAL STAGING RELEASE)\n\n> **Superseded for new deployments:** Use `V16-0-1-MOBILE-BRIDGE-MANAGED-SECRET.md` for current staging setup. This document preserves the original v16.0.0 procedures, which required a runtime secret. v16.0.1 does not require that Cloudflare secret.\n\n**Audience:** Operators who want to test Subsonic/OpenSubsonic clients against WASP while leaving a working v15 production deployment untouched.\n\n## What it is\n\nMobile Bridge is an optional translator built into WASP, not a replacement for the native player, WASP peer protocol, or music database. It accepts compatible Subsonic/OpenSubsonic requests and resolves them against the **staging server's** WASP catalogue, including metadata synchronized from an administrator-added v15 peer. For remote tracks, it streams bytes directly from the source v15 peer on demand. It does **not** import/copy the source's MP3 collection.\n\n**Do not deploy this staging release over your current production worker, GitHub repository, Portainer stack or database.** Use a different repository and a separately named Worker/Portainer stack with isolated persistence. This is a locally tested preview: it has **not** been validated against actual mobile clients, a live Cloudflare deployment, or your particular v15 host.\n\n## Isolation checklist — do this BEFORE first deploy\n\n1. Create a new staging GitHub repository. Extract the **contents** of the release's `WASP-v16.0.0-staging/` folder to the root of that repository. Do not push this to the production `KNDPZ/WASP` main branch.\n2. Confirm `wrangler.jsonc` uses Worker name `wasp-v16-staging` (or your own **unique staging Worker name**) and does **not** target the production Worker. Do not connect the new staging repo to production's Worker build.\n3. Provision **staging-only D1 and R2 resources**, and verify their actual resource IDs/bucket names in the Cloudflare dashboard. The shipped Wrangler configuration intentionally has no production IDs. Do not bind production D1/R2 to this Worker; a separate URL alone does not provide storage isolation.\n4. Add a persistent random secret using Cloudflare Worker secrets, **never in Git**: `npx wrangler secret put MOBILE_BRIDGE_SECRET`. Use 32+ unpredictable bytes, securely generated, retained in a password manager. Replacing/loss of this secret invalidates previously generated encrypted app passwords for legacy token authentication.\n5. On first installation, change WASP's default administrator password **before exposing staging to the internet**, and create a normal test member account. The admin account itself is not a Subsonic member credential.\n6. Keep the v15 source's own storage and webapp unchanged. The only expected interaction is *read-only peer catalogue syncing and on-demand public/shared audio requests* from staging. Ensure the source v15 server has **Share this library on the network** enabled and is reachable from the staging server. Only use music you have permission to host/share.\n\n### Cloudflare Workers deployment\n\n- Connect the separate staging repository to a **new** Cloudflare Worker. In build settings, the repository root is the project root.\n- Set **Deploy command:** `npm run deploy` (runs `npm run build:docs && wrangler deploy`). Do not use production Worker identifiers or the production domain.\n- Confirm your staged D1 (`DB`) and R2 (`MUSIC`) bindings are both present and isolated from v15. A Wrangler auto-provisioning dialog/resource-creation flow may appear, depending on the Cloudflare setup; review every name/ID before acceptance.\n- Set `MOBILE_BRIDGE_SECRET` as a staging Worker secret.\n- Use the unique `*.workers.dev` staging URL during testing, not `https://wasp.playy.online` and not a production alias.\n\n### Docker / Portainer staging\n\n- Use a separate Portainer stack and `docker-compose.yml` in this release. Default host ports are **18080 HTTP / 18443 HTTPS**, so the common production ports 80/443 are not replaced.\n- Persistent Docker volumes are prefixed `wasp-v16-*`. Double-check you don't map production volumes or music folders into the staging container unintentionally.\n- Use a strong persistent `SCANNER_SECRET` or set `MOBILE_BRIDGE_SECRET` in your deployment environment. Staging credentials use it to encrypt legacy app-secret material; changing it affects previously created credentials.\n- Configure HTTPS for mobile login where possible; plain LAN HTTP is not secure for credentials or audio privacy. LAN URLs such as `https://192.168.1.10:18443/mobile` may need a trusted certificate or appropriate hostname.\n\n## Connecting staging v16 to the existing v15 music server\n\n1. On the **v15** source: allow network sharing under Admin → Network and confirm `https://YOUR-V15-HOST/api/peer` returns an `ok` response. Do not modify v15's D1/R2 or music files.\n2. On the **v16 staging** admin interface: Admin → Network → Connected peers → add the **v15** server's normal public HTTPS URL. Do not add the stage server to itself.\n3. Use the **Sync** action for the v15 peer. WASP's existing peer sync copies **metadata only** into staging's `peer_catalogue_chunks`; this is not a file transfer. You may optionally mark the peer as primary for ordinary WASP browsing, but it is **not required** by Mobile Bridge.\n4. Check `https://YOUR-V16-STAGING/mobile` and sign into the staging website with a normal member account. Account Settings → **Mobile** is where members create per-device credentials; users from v15 are *not* automatically cloned to staging.\n5. In staging Admin → General settings, enable **Mobile Bridge** (disabled by default), after checking that the encryption secret is configured. Then create a credential for each phone.\n6. In a Subsonic/OpenSubsonic mobile app set the **Server URL** to `https://YOUR-V16-STAGING/mobile`, **username** to your *staging* WASP username, and **password** to the *generated mobile app secret* (not your normal website password). Apps that support API-key login can use the same generated secret as `apiKey` without a username.\n7. Test `GET /mobile/rest/ping.view` with the app credentials, then browse artists, search, play one v15-sourced track, seek forward, and confirm the source MP3 remains untouched. A `/rest/*` alias is also provided for clients that strip subdirectory prefixes. **Apps differ in how they handle `/mobile`**, so try the alias root URL if a client cannot connect through the prefix.\n\n**Example staging configuration:** `https://YOUR-V16-STAGING.workers.dev/mobile` (example only, not a live deployed URL). **The v15 music source is not the mobile login address.** The unique v16 staging hostname is.\n\n## Supported behavior (v16 staging)\n\n| Area | Status |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/mobile/rest/*.view`, `/rest/*.view`; GET and form POST; JSON and XML | Implemented |\n| API key, per-device legacy app password, salted Subsonic token and hex password | Implemented; separate from main WASP password |\n| Server probe, OpenSubsonic extension discovery, basic user info | Implemented |\n| Artists, albums, songs, search, music-folder browse, album lists | Implemented against staging's local + synced v15 peer metadata |\n| Album artwork | Local or source peer artwork fallback |\n| Streams and downloads | Forward original audio bytes; peer Range requests forwarded; no transcoding |\n| Existing WASP local and synced public peer playlists | Read-only in mobile app (edits not yet exposed) |\n| Stars/favorites | Local staging songs only; peer hearts intentionally not modified |\n| Scrobble / completed plays | Recorded only in **staging** database; source v15 not modified |\n| Lyrics | Placeholder/empty response in this first staging release |\n| Now Playing mobile heartbeat | Not yet integrated; a mobile app's buffer request is not proof of active listening |\n| Genre-specific listings, transcode, audio conversion, playlist mutation | Not yet implemented |\n| Offline downloads | Depends on the mobile app's own caching capability and the source's permitted access |\n| Web player, public Request, Account Settings popup, v15 features | Retained from v15.0.9 starting point |\n\n**Staging catalogue limits:** For bounded memory/DB reads, this first bridge limits the local catalogue to the most recent 6,500 songs, processes up to 15 configured synchronized peers, and reads at most 6,500 entries per peer. WASP peer syncing itself currently limits each track snapshot to 6,000 songs. Larger libraries require additional pagination work before production use.\n\n## Security and privacy notes\n\n- Mobile Bridge is **off by default**. Enable it only after provisioning a staging secret and verifying isolation.\n- Each device gets a separate revocable credential. App secrets are shown **once**; D1 stores a hash and an encrypted copy only for legacy token verification. Never use a WASP website password as an app secret.\n- API keys / Subsonic passwords are bearer credentials. Some mobile clients put them in query parameters, which can appear in proxy access logs; always use HTTPS and avoid logging request query strings.\n- Source peer URLs are selected exclusively from admin-configured, synchronized peers. The bridge does not forward a mobile app's cookies or mobile credentials to v15. It will not follow arbitrary HTTP redirects from peers when proxying streams.\n- The staging bridge cannot magically reach offline, private, or blocked peers. Do not configure untrusted peer targets or circumvent network policies.\n- Source tracks and public playlists remain governed by v15 network sharing. Private v15 user accounts/playlists are not imported.\n- D1 schema additions are non-destructive. Use staging backups anyway. No code rollback can recover accidentally shared/overwritten production storage.\n\n## Validation checklist\n\n- [ ] Original v15 site and player continue unchanged, with its original D1/R2 bindings.\n- [ ] v16 has a separate GitHub repo, Worker, D1, R2, domain, secret, and (for Docker) volumes and ports.\n- [ ] v16 admin version displays `16.0.0`.\n- [ ] The v15 source responds to `/api/peer` and is added/synced from staging.\n- [ ] v16's normal website still plays music and Account Settings popup works.\n- [ ] Mobile Bridge is disabled by default; unavailable before activation.\n- [ ] Authenticated mobile client can ping, browse, search, play, and seek a source v15 song.\n- [ ] Revoking a credential immediately blocks further authenticated access.\n- [ ] Range requests return partial content (`206`) with correct range/length where the source supports them.\n- [ ] Private playlist data, website sessions, and admin credentials are not exposed through mobile APIs.\n- [ ] v15 peer database, music files and playlists are unchanged after mobile playback.\n\n### Rollback\n\nDisable **Mobile Bridge** in staging Admin → General settings or stop the staging Worker/Portainer stack. The v15 production Worker, repository, domain, database, and music files are independent, so no v15 rollback should be necessary.\n\n### Tests in this package\n\n`npm run test:mobile` tests the protocol adapter. `node --test tests/*.test.mjs` runs the backend regression suite. These are **local automated tests**, not confirmation of support for any particular Subsonic mobile app. Test actual devices and your own Cloudflare/Docker staging configuration before considering any production release.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-9-CURRENTLY-STREAMING.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.9 — Currently Streaming and uncapped listener totals\n\nIncremental update on top of **v15.0.8**. This release implements the approved `/#listened` UI designs and caps only the **displayed songs**, not the number of active listeners.\n\n## Listening activity pages\n\n- `/#listened` keeps the **Recently listened** subtab selected. It shows a *single horizontal row* of **Currently Streaming** song cards above the normal listening history. The number of cards changes with available screen width; when overflow exists, the last cell is a circular `•••` **View currently streaming** link.\n- `/#listened/streaming` shows the dedicated **Currently streaming** subtab, with at most **30 distinct active songs**. The newest **started streams** appear first. Heartbeat renewals do *not* reorder the list.\n- Tracks active on several connected servers appear as one card. Listening-history timestamps and the existing pulsing streaming icon remain intact. The history icon can still indicate tracks older than the 30 currently displayed.\n- A red oval at the top-right of the full streaming subtab shows the **total active playback sessions** with a white person icon, directly to the left of the green **LIVE** badge. **30 is not a listener-count limit.** For example, 65 active playback sessions can be represented by 30 cards and a red `65` badge.\n- Browsing between listening subtabs does not unmount the audio element, change the queue, or stop playback. Live activity refreshes around every **5 seconds** while the listening page is open.\n\n## Presence, counting, and privacy\n\n- A session represents one active WASP browser/player playing audio; it is not a unique person or every visitor who simply has WASP open. If one person plays in two tabs, those are two playback sessions. This avoids collecting usernames and personal identifiers.\n- `GET /api/active-streams?summary=1` returns an **independent full active-session count** and up to the 30 newest distinct active songs per server, with start timestamps. The response also contains song keys (without session IDs) to preserve listening-history streaming badges on older currently playing songs.\n- WASP combines server summaries from its configured, reachable peers. Multiple sessions on the same song count separately in the red badge even though the song gets only one card.\n- Peers still running earlier WASP releases return the old array format, so their counts can only be estimated as a lower bound. An unavailable peer also makes totals incomplete. In both cases the badge displays `+` (for example, `12+`) until those peers upgrade or reconnect.\n- The legacy `GET /api/active-streams` **array response stays unchanged**, and Docker's existing allowlisted peer route already permits the new read-only summary query.\n- Sessions disappear on pause, stop or after their 65-second timeout. No raw listener identities, usernames or session IDs are returned by the public summary endpoint.\n\n## Database and deployment\n\nThe existing `active_streams` table is retained and receives one additive `started_at` INTEGER column. The isolated `wasp_live_streams_schema` marker moves from `1` to `2`; the migration is automatic and does not alter the music library, playlists, requests, accounts, play counts, or other migrations. Existing active records continue working; after a track change its fresh start time is recorded.\n\nFor an existing **v15.0.8** installation, back up the application and database, apply the upgrade patch to the existing source tree **without deleting other files**, rebuild/redeploy your Cloudflare Worker or Docker image, and refresh the browser to load `app.js?v=15.0.9` and `style.css?v=15.0.9`. Use the full archive for a fresh copy. Do not delete Docker volumes and do not reset D1.\n\nThe v15.0.8 HTML-backed Account Settings modal and the separate public **Request** page are preserved unchanged. Do not confuse the red total with the number of authenticated users: anonymous listeners are included, and inactive visitors are not.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node --test tests/v15_0_9_streaming_cap.test.mjs`: >30 unique songs, independent listener count, deduplication, newest start order, session heartbeat, expiration, legacy compatibility and privacy.\n- `python tests/ui_v15_0_9_currently_streaming.py`: one-row preview, overflow navigation, 30-song cap, red user counter beyond 30, recent playback indicators, Request visibility and persistent audio.\n- Run the prior `tests/ui_v15_0_8_account_modal.py`, `tests/ui_v15_0_7_recent_live.py`, and full Node suite to catch regressions.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-8-ACCOUNT-SETTINGS-OVERLAY.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.8 — Account Settings without leaving the music\n\nIncremental update to **WASP v15.0.7**. No changes to the music library, D1 tables, uploads, Docker folders, sessions, peer APIs or live-listening heartbeats.\n\n## Account Settings is now a modal\n\n- Clicking **Account settings** in the full/compact navigation or account dropdown opens an accessible native HTML `<dialog>`. The current search, music tiles, playlist, scroll position, current track, play queue and live playback stay mounted behind it.\n- The UI is stored separately in `public/account-settings.html`, which the existing app fetches as a same-origin HTML template and injects into the dialog without an iframe, page refresh or new tab.\n- Two tabs: **Account** (change username with password, change password) and **Publish** (request publishing permission, add peer URL, see status, remove your links). Existing user APIs and permissions are unchanged.\n- The previous red publishing-approval notification badge stays attached to Account Settings in both expanded and compact sidebars; the Publish tab shows a badge until opened.\n- Close using **X**, **Escape**, or the backdrop; reopening retrieves current account information without altering playback.\n- `/#settings`, `/#settings/account`, and `/#settings/publish` legacy links are intercepted and display the modal, retaining the last music-browsing route; new clicks do not change the URL fragment at all.\n- **Request** remains its own public sidebar destination `/#request`, including visitors who are not signed in. Request is NOT included in Account Settings.\n- Administrator account settings remain in the existing `/admin.html#account` management interface. The popup provides an explicit link instead of automatically navigating away.\n- `public/users.html` retains legacy playlist deep-links with the same overlay-capable `app.js` asset version.\n\n## Install\n\nFor existing v15.0.7 installations, back up the application and copy the files from the **v15.0.8 upgrade patch** over the matching paths, *without deleting other files*. Redeploy/rebuild the frontend assets and Worker/Docker application. Reload the browser (the asset query-string cache-bust version is 15.0.8).\n\nFor fresh installs, use the full v15.0.8 archive. The release does **not** require manual database migrations and does not delete existing tracks, users, playlists, listening history, or requests.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `python tests/ui_v15_0_8_account_modal.py`: overlay, route/scroll stability, playback not paused/reloaded, username/password API, Publish controls and notices, guest public Request.\n- `python tests/ui_v15_0_7_recent_live.py`: relative timestamps, anonymous streaming indicator, public Request.\n- `node --test tests/v15_0_5_accounts_publishing.test.mjs tests/v15_0_6_publish_notifications.test.mjs tests/v15_0_7_recent_live.test.mjs`: pre-existing backend regression tests.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-7-LIVE-LISTENING.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.7 — Recently listened timestamps and live playback\n\nThis is an additive update to the v15.0.6 sidebar-notification-fixed baseline.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `/#listened` shows relative play times (for example, `5 mins ago`) using the existing `played_at` history timestamp. The labels refresh while the view remains open.\n- An animated icon based on the supplied streaming image appears beside a song when at least one WASP browser is actively playing that song. Paused/stopped tracks lose the icon after the next refresh.\n- The status is refreshed every 10 seconds from this server and connected peers. Peers still on older releases will show history without live status until upgraded.\n- The **Request** page remains available to logged-out visitors, entirely outside Account Settings. No account-settings modal was implemented in this update.\n\n## Privacy and lifecycle\n\nThe client sends an anonymous in-memory random playback session ID, track ID, optional source peer URL, and playback state. The database stores only those fields and an expiration timestamp. No usernames, account IDs, IP addresses, or browsing details are stored in `active_streams`; the public GET endpoint returns *only* track/source pairs.\n\n`playing` is sent on playback and refreshed about every 25 seconds. `stop` is sent on pause and can be sent on page close. Entries automatically stop appearing 65 seconds after the last heartbeat even if a tab crashes or loses connectivity. Live status is **not** inferred from audio HTTP requests, which can be buffers or prefetches.\n\n## Deployment\n\nFor an existing v15.0.6 installation, apply the upgrade patch without deleting other files, rebuild/redeploy Worker assets or Docker, then reload the browser. The schema migration runs automatically and creates only a new `active_streams` table, an expiry index, and a migration marker. It does not modify tracks, play counts, requests, users, playlists, audio, or settings.\n\nFor an interconnected network, upgrading each participating WASP instance provides the most complete visibility of current playback. The status relies on participating WASP players, not generic external HTTP audio consumers.\n\n## Files updated\n\n`public/app.js`, `public/index.html`, `public/style.css`, `public/streaming.png` (provided image, whose uploaded bytes are a static PNG despite the `.gif` name), `src/index.js`, `docker/peer-relay.mjs`, `package.json`, regression tests, and this documentation.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-6-PUBLISHING-PERMISSIONS-NOTIFICATIONS.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.6 — Publishing Permissions, User Table & Notifications\n\nA focused, additive update to **v15.0.5**, with no change to music-file storage, server-folder mounting, R2 audio objects, or existing admin-managed/Tailscale peers.\n\n## Admin → Users\n\n- New **Allow all registered users, including new accounts, to publish** checkbox, **off by default**. This is a site-wide **default**, not a forced override. Individual permissions always take priority; an administrator can uncheck a user's **Can publish** box to deny that account even when the global default is enabled. Existing explicitly granted/denied permissions are preserved when the global checkbox changes.\n- A **Search username** field filters the Users table in the browser; the request only fetches the accounts with the existing admin-only API.\n- New **Dislike songs** and **Registered date** columns. Column order is Username, Heart songs, Like songs, Dislike songs, Private playlists, Published playlists, Provided links, Online links, Registered date, Can publish, Actions.\n- The first click on a column header sorts ascending (A–Z, low–high, earliest–latest, unchecked–checked). Clicking the same header again reverses the order. **Actions** is not sortable. Sorting and searching operate on the accounts already loaded. A page refresh reloads current counts.\n- An administrator sees a red numbered notification badge next to **Users** whenever users have pending publish requests. Pending requests appear as red dots next to the corresponding usernames. Checking that user's publishing permission approves the request and clears the pending indicators.\n- The **Delete user** button retains the previous administrator-password and exact `delete` double confirmation and now also cleans up outstanding publish requests and approval notices.\n\n## Account settings and Publish requests\n\n- The username fields in **Admin → Account settings** and **Public site → Account settings → Account** use the existing dark, rounded input style.\n- Users who cannot publish see a **Request to Publish** button below the existing explanation. Each account can have only one pending request; repeat submissions are idempotent. After requesting, the page shows an awaiting-approval message instead of another button.\n- The administrator's approval creates a red notification dot next to the user's **Account settings** navigation tab and the **Publish** subtab; when the public sidebar is collapsed the dot is drawn on the top-right of the account icon. Notices are cleared **when the user opens the Publish tab**. Background checks for notifications do not clear them.\n- The administrator's outstanding-request count is checked about every 30 seconds while the admin page is open. The public app checks approval status at the same interval while visible and the user is signed in, as well as during account navigation.\n- **Contributed links always stay peers**. Only administrator-added managed peers can be marked *Treat as local*. The existing public HTTPS-only URL validation and peer health rules remain unchanged.\n\n## Data migration and safety\n\nThe existing `wasp_schema_version` marker moves from **15.2** to **15.3**. Migration only creates two new small tables (`user_publish_requests` and `user_publish_approval_notices`); the global checkbox is a new `settings` entry and defaults to off if missing. Existing v14/v15 repair paths and the `peer_catalogue_chunks` schema repair remain intact. No audio files, existing user accounts, playlists, votes, MP3 tags, Docker volumes, or R2 objects are reset or rewritten.\n\nPending requests and permission notices are private, session-authenticated data. Only administrators may read the pending-request count and the registered-user table. The Admin Users API still omits user-submitted peer URLs; client-side federation still exposes active public peer origins to clients as explained in the previous release notes.\n\n## Deployment\n\n**Changes-only patch**: apply only on top of an unchanged **v15.0.5** checkout, keeping the repository's directory structure. The complete v15.0.6 ZIP is an alternative for a fresh source tree. Back up the production D1 database, R2 objects and persistent Docker volumes, stage the update against an existing v15.0.5 database, then rebuild Docker and deploy Cloudflare from the correct commit. Don't delete any persistent volumes.\n\nVerify **Admin → version** reads `WASP v15.0.6`. Test: a new account inherits the global setting; an explicit per-account denial prevails; a denied account requests access; an admin sees a red badge, grants the request and sees it disappear; the user receives notification dots which disappear after opening Publish. Check username/date/count sorting both ways, text filtering, unchanged delete-user confirmation, and public-peer-only behavior of submitted links.\n\n## Verification scope\n\nBackend tests cover the v15.2-to-v15.3 additive migration, default inheritance, per-user overrides, admin-only permissions, pending-request/approval lifecycle, user acknowledgement, and community-peer classification. Browser tests cover sort/search controls, theme, admin and user badges (including compact icon mode), and the request workflow. No live Docker/Tailscale/Cloudflare deployment is performed as part of this source release.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-5-ACCOUNT-COMMUNITY-PUBLISH.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.5 — Accounts and Community Publishing\n\n## New features\n\n- **Admin → Account settings:** The administrator can change the administrator *username* after entering the current administrator password. Administrator authorization remains tied to the immutable `admin` role in the server-side session, not to the new display/login name. The password and role remain unchanged.\n- **Public site → Account settings → Account:** Signed-in members may change their own username with their current password. Account ID, existing playlists, reactions, and sessions are preserved. Names must be 3–32 letters, numbers, underscores or hyphens; names are unique ignoring case, and the administrator name remains reserved.\n- **Public site → Account settings → Publish:** A signed-in member can list or remove their own WASP peer links if publishing is enabled by an administrator. The permission is **off by default**. Links are never automatically promoted to *Treat as local*; only an entry expressly added by the administrator under **Admin → Network → Connected peers** can become the primary/local source. The same URL, if later added by an administrator, takes precedence in peer discovery.\n- **Admin → Users:** Each account shows a publishing checkbox, number of submitted links and the number last found online; user-submitted URL addresses are not included in this admin page/API response or the administrator's managed-peer list. The admin can delete a member from the far right of the row, after entering the current admin password and the exact word `delete`.\n\n## Link validation and safety\n\nUser-published links must be **public HTTPS hostnames on port 443** (not IP literals, private/Tailscale/local names, HTTP, paths, usernames or custom ports). The server checks DNS A/AAAA records through Cloudflare DNS-over-HTTPS before performing a bounded `/api/peer` probe. The probe has a 6-second timeout, disallows redirects, rejects excessive responses and requires an affirmative WASP peer response with a valid track count. Each member may submit up to eight links. A peer must have sharing enabled on its source WASP server. These restrictions are not applied to the administrator's pre-existing Tailscale or Docker peer entries.\n\n**Network security:** Domain DNS can change between the DNS check and the outbound HTTPS request, so network-level outbound restrictions and egress filtering are recommended for publicly accessible Docker installations. The Cloudflare DNS-over-HTTPS service must be reachable for user link verification. These network integrations have not yet been tested against a live deployment.\n\n**Link privacy limit:** The administrator Users table intentionally exposes *only counts*, not URL addresses. However, public peer playback requires the clients to learn the remote server's origin (through `/api/network/peers` and browser requests). This means a technical administrator—or any client inspecting network traffic—can still discover public contributed peer URLs. Complete secrecy would require a trusted playback relay/proxy and a different architecture.\n\n**Online state:** Links are validated when submitted. Their health is refreshed during Users-page checks (up to eight per load) and the existing peer-check cron when enabled (up to eight per scheduled run). Offline peers are omitted from active public peer discovery and from **Online links** counts after the next successful health check. **Provided links** counts include retained offline records. The online count describes the last completed check, not a continuous connection.\n\n## Deleting an account\n\nThe action requires an existing administrator session, the correct administrator password and exact `delete` confirmation. It removes the member's login, active sessions, user playlists and covers, copied track references in those playlists, recorded signed-in reactions, attributable legacy voter data, submitted peer links and publishing permission. It **does not delete** shared R2 music, files in `/music`, other users' playlists, or the administrator account. The removal of relational account data uses a D1 batch; playlist cover deletion is best-effort.\n\n**Historical attribution limitation:** Older anonymous hearts, likes, song requests, play counts or visitor-token reactions cannot always be associated with a registered account retroactively. v15.0.5 records attribution for new authenticated reactions and submissions so their corresponding records can be removed if the account is deleted. Likewise, if several accounts have shared the same browser visitor token, the system preserves any aggregate reaction shared by another surviving account instead of wrongly deleting that account's contribution. The software does not pretend that old unattributed or shared interactions were fully removed.\n\n## Data migration and deployment\n\nAn additive migration advances `wasp_schema_version` to `15.2` and creates only new tables/indexes: `user_peer_permissions`, `user_published_peers`, and `user_voter_tokens`. It does not reset existing tracks, playlists, peer snapshots or R2 objects. All existing users begin with publishing disabled. The administrator's existing username setting is preserved.\n\nBack up Cloudflare D1/R2 and Docker data volumes before deploying, then stage the new source against a copy of an existing v15.0.4 database. Update Cloudflare and Docker separately as required; a ZIP alone does not deploy either instance. Do not replace or remove any existing Docker volumes. Existing Tailscale Bridge peers remain configured under the administrator's Network page.\n\nVerify that the admin version indicator reads `WASP v15.0.5` and test:\n1. User registration and login; user cannot reach admin endpoints.\n2. Admin and user username changes retain the correct roles and existing account data.\n3. New user publishing remains disabled until explicitly approved.\n4. Public HTTPS WASP link publishes and appears as a **peer**; admin still chooses any primary peer independently.\n5. Online-link counts exclude last-checked-offline entries and the admin Users page does not show link URLs.\n6. Incorrect password or confirmation blocks deletion; correct pair deletes the member's linked data without affecting MP3 files or the admin login.\n\n## Verification scope\n\nBackend tests include the migration, role-safe username changes, publication permissions, URL safety, active peer precedence, online/offline counting, and guarded deletion. Browser tests exercise the actual settings and Users UI. A live Docker build, actual DNS/HTTPS federation, and AWS/Cloudflare production upgrade are **not** verified by these local tests.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-4-COLLAPSIBLE-SIDEBAR.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.4 — Collapsible Sidebar\n\n## Changes\n\n- WASP now starts with an expanded sidebar (unless this browser previously collapsed it). The arrow button at the upper right of the navigation area toggles between the full 240px sidebar and a compact 76px icon rail on desktop. The state is saved locally per browser, not in the user account or database.\n- The icon rail uses the eleven navigation images supplied for this release, placed in rounded-square dark frames; the red heart icon retains its colors, while dark monochrome icons display in light gray for contrast.\n- Hovering a collapsed navigation icon shows a tooltip that follows the pointer and includes its full tab name. Keyboard focus also displays the tooltip and keeps an accessible name for screen readers. The link remains clickable.\n- The sidebar credit/BBCode area is hidden while collapsed and reappears unchanged when expanded. The logo becomes a compact site icon. Main content gains the freed width.\n- At narrow/mobile widths (up to 760px), the existing horizontally scrollable text navigation is retained rather than forcing the icon rail.\n- This is a frontend-only update: no D1 migration, R2 mutation, scanner change, peer rewrite or Docker volume change.\n\n## Deployment\n\nUse the complete package or apply the v15.0.4 patch to the exact v15.0.3 source tree, preserving paths and copying **all eleven** new `public/nav-icons/*.png` files. After updating GitHub, redeploy Cloudflare Workers and rebuild Docker from the same commit if both serve the site. Keep D1/R2 and Docker volumes untouched.\n\n## Verification\n\n1. Sign out: expanded sidebar by default; user-only tabs hidden.\n2. Toggle collapse: rail is narrow, full-width content gains room, all visible tabs remain clickable, tooltips follow the cursor.\n3. Sign in: My playlists and Account settings icons are present; they work.\n4. Add BBCode credits: visible in expanded mode, hidden in compact mode, preserved after reopening.\n5. Reload the page: collapse state persists. Mobile keeps text navigation.\n6. Test music playback and cloud/peer playlist navigation; the sidebar change should not affect these features.\n\nStaging verification is recommended before production; there has been no live Cloudflare/AWS deployment or actual Docker image build in this environment.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-3-EMBEDDED-ARTWORK-FALLBACK.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.3 — Embedded MP3 artwork fallback\n\n## Purpose\n\nPrevent missing online album/artist/track artwork from becoming a large letter tile when a source MP3 contains a usable embedded picture. This is an **additive artwork-only update** to v15.0.2; it does not rewrite MP3s, move files, alter D1 schema, clear playlists, or delete R2 audio.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Cloudflare R2 uploads:** A missing Deezer result now triggers a bounded ID3v2 APIC/PIC extraction from the original uploaded MP3 object. The file is read using R2 range requests. JPEG/PNG/WebP pictures within supported ID3v2.2–2.4 tags are cached as separate `art/track/...`, `art/album/...`, or `art/artist/...` objects. The audio object stays byte-for-byte unchanged.\n- **Already uploaded songs:** Old artwork misses cached as `source=none` no longer prevent the new embedded-cover fallback. A short-lived separate APIC miss marker avoids repeatedly scanning files that truly lack a supported picture.\n- **Docker server folders:** `/music` remains mounted **read-only**. The Docker gateway already extracts ID3 pictures with `music-metadata`; it now also uses those pictures for album and artist art when the online artwork lookup returns no result. Replaced audio files no longer retain a stale previously extracted picture.\n- **Browser/peer tiles:** When artwork for an album, artist, or song fails to load, the page tries embedded cover endpoints for matching tracks, including peer-relayed sources (`/_peer/...`). It falls back to the initial-letter tile only after the available candidates fail.\n- **Cache refresh:** Artwork URL version `artv=15.0.3` avoids old browser-cached 404 artwork results. The application entrypoints also have updated version query strings.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The MP3 must genuinely contain a readable embedded front cover or another matching song must contain one. A file without embedded art will still show a placeholder if online sources also have nothing.\n- The R2 parser supports common ID3v2 APIC/PIC frames and up to approximately 6 MB of leading ID3 metadata; malformed/unsupported picture frames may still be skipped. Docker's `music-metadata` parser handles more music-file formats in `/music`.\n- Other peers should deploy this version to serve their original MP3 covers reliably. A receiving browser can only fall back to image endpoints the source peer makes available.\n- No streaming or Tailscale access configuration is changed in this release.\n\n## Deployment precautions\n\n1. Back up your existing Docker persistent volumes and, if upgrading Cloudflare, your D1 and R2 bindings. **Do not clear them** for an artwork update.\n2. Deploy this code to staging and exercise an uploaded MP3 with APIC artwork, a folder MP3 with APIC artwork, and a peer track through BRIDGE 2.\n3. Docker installations must rebuild from the updated source (the change to `docker/gateway.mjs` is not activated by refreshing a browser).\n4. Verify an online-artwork-miss song now displays the image embedded in its MP3, and verify `/#recent`, album views, artist views, and playback remain functional.\n5. No database migration is required beyond the existing v15.0.2 migration.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node --test tests/*.test.mjs`\n- `python tests/ui_v15_0_3_artwork_fallback.py`\n- `python tests/ui_v15_federation.py`\n- `python tests/ui_v15_0_2_sidebar_bbcode.py`\n\nAll tests use local mocks; this is a staging candidate, **not** a claim of full production, Docker-image, or Cloudflare integration verification.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-2-SIDEBAR-BBCODE.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.2 — Sidebar BBCode credits and compact bottom footer\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is a small, additive follow-up to v15.0.1. It changes **only the optional left-sidebar credits configuration and presentation**, not the music catalogue, music playback, peer connection logic, login, playlists, SHA-256 scanner, or the Docker/Tailscale configuration.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Footer sits at the bottom.** The credits block follows the side navigation and uses available empty space above it. A one-line credit no longer reserves the old fixed 170-pixel iframe height; additional lines naturally extend upward, Spotify-style. If content exceeds available space, the sidebar remains scrollable.\n- **Small, quiet typography.** Credits use 11px text (similar to the Admin version indicator), subtle colors, compact spacing, and optional links and images. The music player remains unobstructed.\n- **BBCode replaces the HTML/CSS/JavaScript editor** at **Admin → General settings → Sidebar credits and branding**. A live preview updates as the administrator types.\n- **The credits renderer uses safe browser DOM methods** instead of injecting the editor text as executable HTML. HTML tags and scripts are never executed. Supported BBCode tags:\n  - `[b]bold[/b]`, `[i]italic[/i]`, `[u]underline[/u]`\n  - `[url=https://github.com/example]GitHub[/url]`, `[url]https://example.com[/url]`\n  - `[img]https://example.com/logo.png[/img]` (image dimensions are constrained by CSS)\n  - `[color=#C8F169]highlight[/color]`, `[center]centered text[/center]`, `[br]`\n- **Link and image URL restrictions.** Only `https://` and same-site paths beginning with `/` are accepted. `javascript:`, `data:`, protocol-relative URLs, arbitrary inline CSS/JS, and unsupported tags cannot turn into active content. Outbound links open in a new tab without referrer credentials.\n- **Saved settings are not erased.** This release introduces the optional `sidebar_bbcode` setting. The older `sidebar_html` setting is retained for backward compatibility/rollback. If a site has not saved BBCode yet, the frontend and admin safely convert supported parts of older saved HTML (text, basic formatting, links and images) into BBCode for display/editing; scripts, styles and embedded content are ignored. A complex legacy HTML footer may therefore look simpler and should be reviewed before the administrator saves new BBCode.\n- **No database schema migration required.** The settings table already supports arbitrary setting keys.\n- **Version and cache update.** The package and Admin version endpoint identify `15.0.2`; public/admin script URLs are updated to discourage stale browser assets.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\n[b]Powered by WASP[/b]\n[url=https://github.com/your-repository]Source code[/url] · [url=https://your-social-page]Follow us[/url]\n[img]https://your-site.example/logo.png[/img]\n```\n\nThe editor is intentionally a **small credits footer**, not a general-purpose advertising/scripting area. The separate **Advertising** section retains its existing isolated HTML/CSS/JavaScript editor and behavior.\n\n## Testing and rollout\n\n1. Back up your current Docker volumes/D1/R2 data and use a staging instance before updating your live Cloudflare Worker or Docker image.\n2. Under Admin → General settings, confirm that saved credits appear in the BBCode editor. Review the preview before saving converted legacy content.\n3. Save a one-line credit and inspect the bottom of the public sidebar. Add several lines; they should expand upward from the same bottom edge. Links should open in a new tab. Test small and large viewports.\n4. For this change, **no file or folder migration and no user data reset** are required. Preserve all existing volumes and databases when rebuilding/redeploying.\n5. Browser security and layout tests cover unsafe links/scripts, legacy HTML conversion, link/image rendering, flexible footer height and admin preview/save. Backend tests check settings persistence and maximum input length.\n\n**Release status:** Local source/testing only. No live Cloudflare/Docker deployment, Docker image build, or real production migration was performed.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-1-FEATURE-COMPLETION.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.1 — Feature completion and deployment audit\n\n## What changed since v15.0.0\n\nThis is an **additive staging correction**, not a fresh installation or a data reset. The v15.0.0 ZIP contained the feature code, but was never automatically installed on either Cloudflare or Docker. A source ZIP alone does not change a deployed application.\n\n- **Schema repair for already marked-v15 installations.** Upgrade now checks and creates the missing v14/v15 peer sync, listening and fingerprint tables on databases marked `14`, `14.2`, `14.3` **or `15`**, then advances the migration marker to `15.1` when the operations succeed. Existing tables, accounts, music, playlists and R2 objects are not cleared. Existing `15.1` databases take the fast path.\n- **Primary peer wins during record deduplication.** When the same recording is available in local R2 and the selected primary peer, the selected peer is the visible/playing catalogue source. Other physical sources remain alternatives; audio is never downloaded or moved merely by selecting the peer. Primary-peer playlist detail pages now navigate back to **Server playlists**, not Peer playlists.\n- **Keep a last synced catalogue when a peer is temporarily unavailable.** If a peer revision is newer but a live download fails, WASP may show the last saved song/playlist metadata rather than an empty category. Music still needs its source online to play; stale metadata is not proof of playback availability. If an identical playable track exists locally, that local copy is preferred while the designated primary peer is offline.\n- **Stale duplicate fingerprints removed from the database.** At the start of each SHA-256 scan, the Docker scanner refreshes the file inventory and prunes obsolete fingerprint entries for files no longer present. The operation never deletes MP3/audio files or existing track/playlist metadata in `/music`.\n- **Safer duplicate cleanup.** When the only other matching copy is in `/music`, the administrator must complete a fresh scan before deleting an unreferenced, unplayed managed upload. A scan older than five minutes does not authorize such deletion. Do not remove original music files or Docker volume mounts. The manual delete option can remain unused.\n- **Asset cache distinction.** HTML module entrypoints now request `app.js?v=15.0.1` or `admin.js?v=15.0.1` to reduce old-client confusion after new deployments.\n\n## Where each requested v15 feature is found\n\n| Feature | Where it appears / prerequisites |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fix `peer_catalogue_chunks` | Automatic additive database migration on the **newly deployed** worker/container; check cloud sync afterward |\n| Docker HTTPS | `docker/Caddyfile` copied by Dockerfile; set `SITE_ADDRESS` and `DEFAULT_SNI` to the actual hostname/IP and rebuild the container; trusted HTTPS requires a valid trusted certificate |\n| MP3 upload hashing | **Admin → Add music**; works in browser secure contexts (trusted HTTPS / localhost); HTTP public IP shows a clear diagnostic instead of silently failing |\n| SHA-256 scanner, duplicate counter and guarded deletion | **Admin → Duplicate management**; Docker `/music:ro` only; click **Scan music folder now** or enable a schedule under **Network → Crons**; default schedule is off |\n| Treat one peer as local | **Admin → Network → Connected peers**, radio selector; choose one and reload the public site; the selected peer must be reachable and share its catalogue |\n| Metadata peer sync / scheduled peer sync | Connected peers **cloud-sync** or **Refresh** button; **Network → Crons → Check connected peers → Sync peer catalogues and playlists** (off until enabled) |\n| Recently listened without timestamps | Public site `/#listened` (newest first; title, artist, play counts) |\n| Text/image site name | **Admin → General settings → Branding → Top-left site name format**; upload an image and save the branding choice |\n| Custom bottom-left sidebar | **Admin → General settings → Sidebar credits and branding**, below Branding and above Advertising; empty/disabled by default, sandboxed iframe |\n| GitHub-style documentation viewer | **Admin → Update documentations**, below the separator and above **View site**. Admin access only; Markdown is compiled during deployment. |\n\n## Why features may not appear yet\n\n1. **Version mismatch:** the previous complete v15.0.0 ZIP was a **staging artifact**; it did not update your live containers or Cloudflare Worker. In the admin sidebar, verify the reported version is `15.0.1`. If not, the server is still running older code.\n2. **Old Docker image:** a Portainer repository edit or GitHub push alone may not rebuild the image. Rebuild using the correct branch/commit and **keep every existing data volume**, especially `/app/.wrangler/state`, `/data`, `/config`, `/music`, and any uploads storage. Never run `docker compose down -v` for this upgrade.\n3. **Build-generated documentation:** Cloudflare deployment should run `npm run deploy` (which runs `npm run build:docs`); Docker builds the Markdown bundle automatically. Do not publish `docs/` as a public web directory.\n4. **Configuration:** SHA-256 hashing and peer-sync cron are **disabled by default**. Select a primary peer explicitly. The Cloudflare Worker cannot directly reach a `100.x.x.x` private Tailscale address or a Docker peer that is not securely exposed over a publicly reachable endpoint. Docker can use the existing Tailscale Bridge from its own network.\n5. **Old browser assets:** after the correct server version is active, do a hard reload once. Cached frontend files from an older release will not contain new navigation sections.\n6. **Two storage locations:** `/music` is a read-only host folder for scanner indexing; web uploads still use the local R2 emulation on Docker or R2 on Cloudflare. Fingerprint scanning does not migrate audio to/from the peer.\n\n## Operational and rollback checklist\n\n1. Back up both D1 databases, Cloudflare R2 data, Docker application state volumes, server music folders, Portainer stack settings, and the current image identifier.\n2. Stage v15.0.1 against a **copy** of a v14.0.3 or v15.0.0 database; verify that it repairs missing `peer_catalogue_chunks` without modifying user, playlist or track counts.\n3. Confirm the admin version label and inspect the nine features above, including the new admin documentation viewer.\n4. On Docker, connect your approved Bridge, select it as the primary peer, run one manual metadata sync, and check songs, artist labels and server/public playlists. Test a playable remote track.\n5. For duplicates, scan a small `/music` test folder first. Verify the file hashes and that original files remain unchanged. Run a second scan to verify unchanged files are skipped. Deletion must reject songs with plays, reactions or playlist references.\n6. For HTTPS, use the correct IP/hostname in `SITE_ADDRESS` and `DEFAULT_SNI` and verify with a trusted HTTPS browser. `TLS_ARGS=internal` does **not** provide public browser trust by itself.\n7. If an upgrade has problems, roll back only the app image/repository version while keeping data volumes intact. Never reset production D1/R2 to undo an application change without an approved recovery plan.\n\n### Still out of scope\n\n- Automatically exposing a private Tailscale-only Docker host to a public Cloudflare Worker. This requires a separately deployed, secured connectivity method (such as a Cloudflare Tunnel or authenticated HTTPS proxy).\n- Downloading/migrating the full R2 music library to a dedicated machine or removing R2. This is the longer-term storage migration and is not implemented by the primary-peer display option.\n- Safe automatic deletion of original `/music` files. The source folder remains **read-only**, and WASP intentionally refuses that action.\n\n## Verification\n\nLocal Node/SQLite backend tests, browser mocks and JavaScript/shell syntax checks are used for this release. Tests are not equivalent to an actual Cloudflare D1/R2 deployment, container build, Portainer upgrade, or live Bridge/Tailscale streaming. Treat the package as a staging candidate until those checks are completed.\n"},{"name":"V15-0-0-FEDERATED-LIBRARY.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.0 — Federated library, storage audit, and admin improvements\n\n## Release status\n\n**Staging candidate.** The code and automated tests were run locally. Docker image construction, production Cloudflare D1/R2, AWS/Tailscale Bridge, cross-server streaming, and a live production upgrade have **not** been exercised in this environment. Back up and stage before deploying. No production data was changed to prepare this release.\n\n## Upgrade first: repair broken v14.0.3 migrations\n\nv14.0.3 could mark a schema as `14.3` while failing to create `peer_catalogue_chunks` because multiple SQL strings were passed into a single-argument `DB.prepare()` helper. The v15 upgrade checks that earlier marker and independently creates missing tables, including `peer_catalogue_chunks`, `peer_catalogue_cache`, `recent_listens`, and `file_fingerprints`. Existing track records, user accounts, playlists, R2 objects, scanner volumes, and admin settings are not reset. The schema marker advances to `15` after required statements succeed. The old v14 D1 parameter-limit optimization remains in place.\n\n## Primary peer as local\n\nUnder **Admin → Network → Connected peers**, one peer may be selected using a radio button as **Treat as local**; choose **None** to disable it. Only one peer is selected at a time; removing that peer also clears the selection. The selected peer's playable tracks, server playlists, public user playlists, and artists are shown under the local browsing categories and **In your library** labels rather than Peer Library. Tracks retain their true origin and are streamed from the source, without downloading audio. Playlist IDs remain namespaced to avoid collisions.\n\n**Network limitation:** a Cloudflare Worker cannot directly fetch a private Tailscale address. To make a private Docker server the public Cloudflare website's primary source, expose a suitably secured and reachable endpoint (such as a properly configured Cloudflare Tunnel/reverse proxy) and validate artwork, audio, account/privacy, CORS and playlist behavior. This release does **not** implement that secure outbound publishing/tunnel, automatic R2 migration, or R2 removal. Metadata-only snapshots are available where the current peer-synchronization workflow succeeds.\n\n## SHA-256 and duplicate management\n\n**Admin → Duplicate management** displays progress, exact-byte duplicate counts, source paths and safe deletion controls. The scanner works on Docker only, reads the configured `/music` folder, hashes audio as a stream, and sends only fingerprints and metadata to the local Wrangler/D1 database. The existing `/music:ro` mount remains read-only. The hashing operation does not rewrite tags, modify filenames, move or delete audio. Previously fingerprinted, unchanged files are skipped using size and modification time. Enable a schedule in **Network → Crons → SHA-256 hash local /music folder** (off by default) or request a scan manually. Hashing a large library uses disk read bandwidth: plan it for a quiet period.\n\nUploads compare their supplied SHA-256 fingerprint against uploaded files **and** already-indexed folder fingerprints. Exact duplicate groups may include multiple different paths. Different encodings, modified tags, or different MP3 bytes do not have the same SHA-256; acoustic fingerprinting is not included.\n\n**Deletion limitations and safeguards:** the administrator must explicitly review and confirm. The app never deletes a file in `/music`. Only an uploaded object with an identical surviving fingerprint can be deleted, and only if it has no playlist references, reactions, or plays. Folder scan evidence must be recent to justify deleting a duplicate against an original in `/music`. Existing records are not automatically deduplicated or rewritten; other user data remains intact. **Back up first.** You can keep all duplicates if desired.\n\n## Permanent Docker HTTPS configuration\n\nThe deployed Docker image uses `docker/Caddyfile`; both that file and the root `Caddyfile` now explicitly configure `https://{$SITE_ADDRESS:localhost}` and proxy the Docker gateway on `127.0.0.1:8788`. Set `SITE_ADDRESS` to the actual IP/hostname used to connect, **without a protocol or port**, and set `DEFAULT_SNI` to match in Docker Compose/Portainer. HTTP on 8080 and HTTPS on 8443 remain in one container. The corrected image persists across reboots and redeployments without a host-specific bind mount. A rebuilt image is required to pick up this repository change.\n\n`TLS_ARGS=internal` (the existing default) uses Caddy's private certificate authority. This encrypts traffic but is not automatically trusted by browsers; use an appropriate certificate trust setup or a public hostname with a correctly configured publicly trusted certificate for public production deployments. Merely clicking through a browser warning is not the same as a trusted HTTPS environment. The upload UI now displays a clear message when `crypto.subtle` is unavailable due to HTTP or untrusted context.\n\n## Recently listened\n\nThe newest 50 confirmed listening events remain in reverse chronological order and update approximately every 10 seconds using local and reachable peer feeds. The UI now shows artwork, title, artist, and play count, without displaying date or time. Private listeners' usernames and IP addresses are not published.\n\n## Branding and sidebar credits\n\nUnder **Admin → General settings → Branding**, switch the top-left site name between text and a custom raster image. Branding image, browser tab name, and favicon are independent. A new **Sidebar credits and branding** section below Branding and above Advertising accepts optional HTML/CSS/JavaScript for credits, links, images, badges, or powered-by notices. It is blank and disabled by default. Custom markup executes in a sandboxed iframe without same-origin permission or access to application authentication; public settings can be cached by browsers.\n\n## Update documentations\n\nAn admin-only **Update documentations** navigation item is below the sidebar separator, immediately above View site. It presents release notes from `docs/*.md` with GitHub-like Markdown formatting, tables, headings, links and code blocks. Sources are bundled into `src/release-docs.js` using `npm run build:docs` rather than being published as public static Markdown files. Only authenticated admins can retrieve documents through `/api/admin/docs`. This is a read-only viewer.\n\n## Deployment / acceptance checklist\n\n1. Back up Cloudflare production D1/R2 and the Docker local D1 state, `/data/uploads`, `/music` source, Caddy state and settings/Portainer stack definition. Record current version and container/image digest. Do not clean a database or delete volumes.\n2. Stage the **same v15 source** on a nonproduction Docker stack or Worker and a copied database first. Confirm that a v14.0.3 DB with marker `14.3` but no `peer_catalogue_chunks` table repairs itself and retains playlists/users.\n3. Check `/api/peer`, `/api/tracks`, `/api/peer-catalogue`, `/api/playlists`, and the new **Duplicate management** admin page. Try sync and refresh. Confirm the radio primary selection changes the local playlist/artist labels and that the original audio streams from the peer.\n4. Configure `SITE_ADDRESS`, `DEFAULT_SNI`, and certificate trust. Verify TLS and MP3 upload on a trusted HTTPS browser connection. **No special AWS UDP port is necessary for the HTTPS TCP service.**\n5. Keep the SHA-256 cron disabled initially, scan a few controlled files manually, review unchanged contents/hashes and database progress, then enable a suitable schedule. Verify the duplicate counter and confirm that any file with playlist references is refused deletion.\n6. Test branding, the isolated sidebar iframe, admin documentation permissions, user access, listening activity, playlist covers, copied playlists, and Most listened before migrating production traffic.\n7. Roll back the **application image only** if needed. The additive schema tables may remain in place; do not roll back by restoring/deleting the entire live database unless a deliberate tested recovery plan requires it.\n\n## Automated verification performed\n\nBackend API tests and browser regressions run against in-memory SQLite mocks and Chromium. They do not simulate Cloudflare networking, full Docker image build, cloud certificate issuance, Portainer, or production loads. Streaming and live cross-peer behavior still need staging verification.\n"},{"name":"V14-D1-OPTIMIZATION.md","content":"# WASP v14 — Cloudflare D1 Optimization\n\nThis release focuses on the main Cloudflare-hosted WASP deployment (`music.playy.online`). It is based on the complete WASP v13 Artist & Playlist Reactions release. **Bridge/Nginx caching is intentionally deferred.**\n\n## Why this release exists\n\nProduction Cloudflare D1 Query Insights showed approximately 6 million rows read in a 24-hour view. Four queries resembling `SELECT artist, artists FROM tracks WHERE instr(...) ...` accounted for about 5.22 million of those reads. An `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` run on the live D1 database confirmed `SCAN tracks`; the database had indexes for the primary key and track hash, but not artist or album lookups.\n\nThese are *observations from the supplied dashboard*, not a measured before-and-after benchmark. We cannot promise a particular reduction until a staging and production comparison has been performed.\n\n## Changes in v14\n\n### 1. Indexed artist membership\n\n- Adds a non-destructive `track_artist_map` table, with `(track_id, artist_key)` primary key and `idx_track_artist_lookup(artist_key, album)` index.\n- Populates it once from the existing `tracks` metadata, using the same multi-artist parsing already used throughout WASP. This includes collaborations and protected band names such as `AC/DC`.\n- Replaces repeated `instr(artist, ?)` / `instr(artists, ?)` whole-library scans in `libraryHasArtist()` with indexed lookups.\n- Keeps the map in sync on web uploads, local-folder scanning, track edits, deletion, and full-library cleanups. Original `tracks` records and audio objects are preserved during migration.\n- Adds an index on `tracks(album)` and ordering indexes for playlist-track tables.\n\n**First migration:** The new mapping and indexes require a one-time read of the existing catalogue and some D1 writes. WASP records `wasp_schema_version = 14` so normal requests and future Worker instances no longer rerun v13's CREATE/ALTER statements. A D1-backed short-lived migration lease prevents simultaneous backfills by many Worker isolates; while it runs, some requests might briefly need to retry. The migration is not a music transfer or an R2 rewrite.\n\n### 2. Regional Cloudflare public response cache\n\nOnly these exact, public, session-independent **GET** endpoints are cached by the Worker Cache API:\n\n| Route | Edge TTL |\n|---|---:|\n| `/api/tracks` | 90 seconds |\n| `/api/playlists` | 120 seconds |\n| `/api/user-playlists` (public only) | 60 seconds |\n| `/api/site` | 120 seconds |\n| `/api/entity-reactions` (public aggregates) | 5 seconds |\n| `/api/track-reaction-counts` (new public aggregates) | 5 seconds |\n\nThe cache is checked *before* D1 initialization or the sharing-setting query. A warm cache hit can therefore serve the JSON without any D1 request. Cache entries use a versioned key, and selected admin mutations invalidate affected entries on a best-effort basis. Entries also expire after their TTL; updates may take up to the TTL to become visible in another data center.\n\n**Never shared-cached:** authentication, private playlists, accounts, passwords, admin requests, session-specific reaction-state, request queues, personal histories, and any write requests. The cache is regional and Cloudflare may evict it at any time. These caches **reduce D1 queries on hits but do not bypass Worker invocation**; separate Workers Caching is not enabled because this application has routes requiring strict private authorization.\n\nInspect the `x-wasp-cache` response header on a public route (`MISS` or `HIT`) when the Cache API is available; cache hits are not guaranteed when consecutive requests land at different data centers.\n\n### 3. Smaller periodic reaction refresh\n\nWhen a visitor is viewing reaction charts, the browser now loads `/api/track-reaction-counts` rather than re-downloading the full `/api/tracks` music catalogue every 15 seconds. Personal vote state still comes from `/api/reaction-state` and `/api/peer-reactions`, and immediate optimistic voting remains as in v13.\n\n### 4. Safe rollout and compatibility\n\nThe new schema is additive. The only new table is `track_artist_map`, plus new indexes and the schema-version/lease settings rows. Existing tracks, R2 music, users, playlist contents, reactions, peer metadata, Docker volumes and settings remain intact. Rolling back to v13 code leaves the new table unused; it is not necessary to delete it.\n\n## Recommended staged rollout\n\n1. **Back up production first.** Use the Cloudflare D1 Time Travel bookmark/export or another tested backup method. Do not clear your D1 database or R2 bucket.\n2. Deploy the v14 ZIP into a **separate staging Worker with separate D1 and R2 bindings**. Avoid pushing untested v14 code straight to the Git-connected production branch.\n3. Populate staging with a representative catalogue (and playlist/reaction data where permitted). Run the bundled tests using Node 22+ and optional Playwright.\n4. After deployment, the first request creates the indexes and artist map. Verify `PRAGMA index_list('track_artist_map');` and `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT 1 FROM track_artist_map WHERE artist_key = 'ac dc' LIMIT 1;` (should indicate indexed SEARCH rather than `SCAN tracks`).\n5. Verify searching and opening local and peer artists, biographies, similar artists, playlist playback, and reactions. Check `GET /api/tracks` response for `x-wasp-cache`; validate repeated reads and cache invalidation on admin edits.\n6. Deploy on production **after the daily D1 quota reset**, ideally at a low-traffic time, only after staging succeeds. Watch Worker error rates and D1 Metrics / Query Insights. A one-time migration can incur extra rows read/written at deploy time.\n7. Compare rows read per 24 hours or per 1,000 HTTP visits across similar traffic windows. Focus on the disappearance of repeated `instr()` artist queries in Query Insights and measure actual cache hit behavior.\n\n## Run local tests\n\n```bash\nnode tests/v14_d1_optimization.test.mjs\nnode tests/api_smoke.mjs\nnode tests/v13_entity_reaction_playlists.test.mjs\nnode tests/gateway_proxy.test.mjs\n# Optional with Python Playwright + Chromium:\npython tests/ui_smoke.py\n```\n\n## Known limits / not included\n\n- Full live AWS EC2 Docker image and live Cloudflare streaming were not tested in this packaging environment.\n- A D1 Cache API hit is specific to the data center handling the request. Cold caches and new unique requests still execute SQL.\n- Newly added track/album/playlist metadata may remain stale on other edges for up to the short public TTL. Admin mutations proactively evict in the handling location.\n- This release does not add Nginx caching to the Tailscale Bridge and does not migrate data away from Cloudflare D1/R2.\n- The first on-demand v14 migration is a one-time write operation. Avoid simultaneous manual D1 schema edits while deploying.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-3-PEER-SYNC-RECENT-LISTENED.md","content":"# WASP v14.0.3 — Peer metadata sync and Recently listened\n\n## Main changes\n\n**Admin → Network → Connected peers** now offers two actions on each peer:\n\n- **Refresh:** verifies the peer and then performs a fresh metadata sync (tracks, server playlists, and public user playlists). Unlike the old status-only refresh, this actively reloads the catalogue and publishes a local revision. The app's other open tabs receive a refresh signal and update automatically.\n- **☁ ↻ Cloud sync:** saves a new copy of the same peer metadata directly to the receiving WASP installation's D1 database, without downloading music files or image binaries. The sync interface shows actual completed stages (40%, 70%, 95%, 100%) above its status notification.\n\nThe source peer remains authoritative for playback. A snapshot stores track metadata, source identifiers, public playlist definitions, and artwork URLs, not the audio. If a snapshot becomes stale compared to a peer catalogue revision, the browser can fetch the newer live catalogue through the existing Docker peer relay.\n\n**Recently listened** is a new left-navigation page after Most listened. It displays up to 50 latest confirmed song plays with title, artist, cover, time, and play count; newest first. It combines this WASP installation's own history with read-only anonymous activity published by reachable, connected WASP peers. Updates are polled about every ten seconds; an edge cache can add up to roughly ten seconds of staleness. No listener names, IP addresses, or session identifiers are shared through the endpoint.\n\n**Admin → Network → Crons → Check connected peers for new tracks** includes a new *Sync peer catalogues and playlists* checkbox. It uses the existing enabled frequency/timezone schedule. All cron jobs remain disabled by default unless the administrator enables them.\n\nThe admin-only bottom-left version reads **WASP v14.0.3 · Peer Sync & Recently Listened**.\n\n## Non-destructive database upgrade\n\nThe schema marker advances from `14.2` to `14.3` and adds:\n\n- `peer_catalogue_cache` and `peer_catalogue_chunks` (peer metadata snapshots)\n- `recent_listens` and `idx_recent_listens_time` (anonymous recent play activity)\n\nD1 has a two-million-byte maximum row/string size. Snapshot records are chunked to approximately 240 KB each, well below that limit. A sync updates only the receiving server's metadata tables. It never modifies the source peer's music files or deletes user/admin playlist tables.\n\nPreviously played songs cannot be recovered into the new activity feed because historical individual play timestamps were not recorded. The feed starts accumulating after v14.0.3 deployment.\n\n## Cross-server limitations\n\n1. Both a receiving server and any source whose recent activity should be displayed must run WASP v14.0.3 or newer, since older Workers do not provide `/api/recent-listens`.\n2. The Docker server can read Cloudflare recent activity through its existing Nginx/Tailscale Bridge and same-origin relay. The public Cloudflare Worker cannot automatically reach a private-only Docker server, so Docker-only listening events cannot appear in Cloudflare's feed without an additional, secure and reachable publishing path.\n3. A stale metadata snapshot can keep a peer's cards visible while it is temporarily offline; **audio playback still requires a reachable source peer**. Syncing metadata does not host the audio locally.\n4. Rapid repeated manual syncs use D1 writes. Choose a conservative cron frequency, typically daily, and monitor D1 rows written as well as rows read.\n5. For very large or unusually verbose catalogues, syncing is bounded to an 8 MB response per collection and at most 6,000 tracks per peer in this release. Exceeding the limit returns an error; it does not delete source music.\n\n## Suggested staging rollout\n\n1. Back up both deployments' D1 databases and preserve R2 buckets, volumes, and Portainer mappings.\n2. Deploy the ZIP to a staging Cloudflare Worker with separate test resources. Then deploy the updated Docker source via a test Portainer stack.\n3. Add the public Cloudflare domain through the existing Bridge peer and click **☁ ↻** or **Refresh**. Confirm progress reaches 100%, that new tracks, new artists, and both types of public playlists appear, and that playback still uses the original peer.\n4. Play a song through Cloudflare for at least ten seconds, open **Recently listened** on Docker, and confirm it appears (allow a brief polling/cache delay).\n5. Enable the sync checkbox under the peers cron only after manual sync has been verified.\n6. Monitor Cloudflare D1 query and rows-written metrics to ensure periodic jobs are not causing excessive usage.\n\nNo new Docker volume mount, Tailscale exit node, or Nginx Bridge configuration is required.\n\n## Validation performed locally\n\n- JavaScript syntax checks\n- Node API regression suite, including admin authorization, D1 migration, metadata chunking for 1,400 tracks, and anonymous recent listening\n- Playwright browser checks for progress, scheduled sync checkbox, snapshot rendering, new peer revisions, and peer activity\n- ZIP integrity check\n\n**Not verified:** a complete Docker image build, a live Cloudflare D1 migration, or streaming between your production Docker, Bridge and Cloudflare servers. This is a staging candidate, not a production-verified release.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-2-LIBRARY-REFRESH-CRONS.md","content":"# WASP 14.0.2 — Catalogue freshness, listening stats, scheduled checks\n\nThis is an **additive staging release**, built on WASP v14.0.1 (the D1 SQL parameter-limit hotfix). It is not a replacement database, and it is not a new music library.\n\n## 1. New songs, new artists, and granted playlists\n\n- Successful changes to the local catalogue, administrator playlists, or public user playlists advance one D1 `settings.catalog_revision` value. Grants of playlist requests also advance the revision.\n- Browsers that remain open check this inexpensive version value approximately every 30 seconds while visible, instead of fetching the entire track catalogue every time.\n- On a revision change, the browser reloads tracks and playlists using a revision-specific cache key. Public Cloudflare edge caches can still reduce D1 reads between changes, while updated records are immediately available on the new cache key.\n- A Docker peer also exposes the revision over its same-origin relay; the browser checks the connected peers and refreshes when their catalogues change.\n- Uploaded artists are added to the existing normalized artist index without a full rebuild. After successful uploads, background tasks try the existing online biography, artist portrait, and album cover lookups. Missing or unavailable third-party information does not block uploads. The result is cached using existing D1/R2 records.\n- **Content freshness is normally within the next ~30-second visible-page check; it is not a real-time push.** If a tab is hidden or a peer is offline, it may refresh later.\n\n## 2. Most listened — local and connected peers\n\n- Docker now records a listening event for a peer-hosted track after **10 seconds of actual playback**, as it already does for local music.\n- The local Docker Worker stores these counts in a small new `peer_listens` table; it never deletes or edits original peer audio.\n- A public `/api/peer-plays` GET publishes **unsynced** counts only when network sharing is enabled. The browser aggregates source-host play counts and reachable connected-peer summaries.\n- With `PEER_PLAY_SYNC=1` (Docker default), Docker additionally attempts to POST each completed peer listen to the original peer's existing `/api/play/<track-id>` endpoint through the configured Bridge. A successful source acknowledgment is recorded in `synced_plays`; this event will then be excluded from `/api/peer-plays` so it is not counted twice. If the network POST fails, the event remains counted locally.\n- Set `PEER_RELAY_ALIASES` to a JSON map of public peer origin to local Bridge origin when using a Tailscale proxy, e.g. `{\"https://example-worker.example\":\"http://100.100.100.100:8081\"}`. The actual origin of a stored peer should still be the Bridge URL.\n- **Limit:** Docker-only tracks cannot automatically appear in Cloudflare's Most listened if the Cloudflare Worker cannot reach the Docker server and Docker does not securely publish that track metadata. For music originally hosted on Cloudflare, Docker's successful play reports update the source-host play counter, so those plays appear in Cloudflare rankings. Wider cross-origin aggregation requires a separate authenticated outbound publishing design or an externally reachable trusted HTTPS peer.\n\n## 3. Admin → Network → Crons\n\nOpen **Admin → Network → Crons**. This tab matches the existing WASP admin theme and is visible only to administrators.\n\nEach checkbox is disabled by default:\n\n1. Check connected peers for new tracks (compares the number of tracks advertised by each reachable peer and updates its status/track count).\n2. Fetch missing artist and album artwork.\n3. Fetch artist biographies and lineups.\n\nConfigure the timezone once (IANA timezone, e.g. `Asia/Manila`). Every job can run:\n\n- **Daily** — at a chosen 24-hour `HH:MM` local time.\n- **Weekly** — select Sunday through Saturday; executes at **00:00** local time on that day.\n- **Monthly** — select day **1 through 30**; executes at **00:00** local time on that day. Months lacking the selected day skip it.\n\nCloudflare executes a UTC scheduled Worker trigger once a minute, and WASP interprets the selected timezone inside the handler. Docker uses a local one-minute scheduler and authenticates it with its persistent scanner secret. Each job is guarded against running repeatedly within the same scheduled minute. Artwork/biography jobs process a bounded batch of up to eight newer tracks per tick, not the whole library.\n\n**Caution:** Scheduling more frequent checks increases API calls, Cloudflare Worker invocations and potentially D1 queries. Keep schedules disabled until the deployment is stable; then start with one daily schedule, observe metrics, and enable others as needed.\n\nThe Cron check only refreshes peer status and catalogue metadata. It does not download, move, or delete original music files.\n\n## 4. Admin-only version indicator\n\nThe bottom-left of `/admin` and all admin hash pages reads the running application's version from `/api/admin/version` (administrator authentication required):\n\n`WASP v14.0.2 · Library Refresh & Peer Listening`\n\nThe main listener website does not show the build version. Checking the badge after a Portainer rebuild can help distinguish stale images from a deployed source revision.\n\n## 5. Data preservation, deployment and rollback\n\n- **Back up production D1** (Cloudflare Time Travel or a verified SQL export) and note the last working Worker deployment before upgrading.\n- Deploy to a separate staging Worker with separate D1 and R2 resources where possible. Do not point staging at the live R2 bucket or D1 database.\n- Upgrade v14.0.1 to v14.0.2 adds the `peer_listens` table and an index on `tracks.created_at` and changes a schema marker. It does not truncate `tracks`, `playlists`, `playlist_tracks`, `user_playlists`, `reactions`, `artist_info`, or other existing records. It does not delete or move R2 objects.\n- Do **not** restore D1 to an earlier time just to roll back the Worker. Roll back the Worker deployment code if needed. Additional v14.0.2 tables are additive and can remain while using the previous Worker.\n- When updating Docker in Portainer, **retain** existing volumes, upload data, state and music-folder bindings. Do not use `docker volume prune` or delete the stack's volumes.\n- The source ZIP does not include `.env` to avoid overwriting site-specific credentials or host volume paths. Configure production env vars in Portainer / Cloudflare Secrets as appropriate.\n- Cloudflare Cron Triggers are declared in `wrangler.jsonc`. They may take time to propagate. All three application jobs start **disabled** even though the minute trigger is configured.\n- Docker image/build tests and live Cloudflare/Tailscale playback were **not** performed in the development environment. These remain staging/production acceptance checks.\n\n## 6. Acceptance checks\n\n1. Upload a single track that has a new artist. Confirm its song and artist appear in a fresh `/#recent` and Artists view; then leave a second tab open, and verify it reloads within a polling interval while visible.\n2. Grant a playlist request or create an admin playlist. Verify it appears in `/#playlists` without clearing browser caches or resetting the Worker.\n3. Check online biography and cover lookup for the newly uploaded artist; external sources may have no data.\n4. Play a peer-origin track through Docker for more than ten seconds. Verify the local Most listened chart; confirm the source Cloudflare `plays` value also grows when `PEER_PLAY_SYNC=1` and the source `/api/play` POST is reachable through Bridge.\n5. Configure a safe test cron schedule. Confirm saving, timezone handling and one run per selected minute. Turn it back off after verification.\n6. Check `Admin → Network` still displays connected peers, `/admin` displays the current version, and no existing administrator or user playlists have disappeared.\n7. Monitor D1 rows read/written for 30–60 minutes after upload completion and after active browsing; do not compare an upload-heavy interval with a quiet interval without accounting for traffic.\n\n## Test coverage\n\nThe packaged project includes the existing Worker, Docker gateway, browser, and reaction tests, plus `tests/v14_0_2_refresh_crons_peerplays.test.mjs` and `tests/ui_v14_0_2_refresh_crons.py`. They cover catalogue revision invalidation, new playlist visibility, local and synced peer-play counting, cron access controls, weekly/monthly local-time triggering and duplicate suppression, authenticated admin version UI, and the additive upgrade from schema version 14.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-1-D1-HOTFIX.md","content":"# WASP v14.0.1 — Emergency Cloudflare D1 hotfix\n\n## Incident and cause\n\nThe original v14 backfill used 35 mapping rows per `INSERT`, with three bound parameters per mapping: **105 bound parameters**, exceeding Cloudflare D1's **100-bound-parameter** limit. On a library with enough tracks, the initial migration fails with `D1_ERROR: too many SQL variables`, returning HTTP 500 for the Worker and preventing peer discovery. This was missed in the old small-fixture tests.\n\n## Exact correction\n\n`src/index.js`: batch `track_artist_map` backfill in groups of **30 rows / 90 bound parameters**. An additional regression test `tests/v14_d1_parameter_limit.test.mjs` simulates the Cloudflare bound-parameter limit while migrating 155 tracks (310 artist mappings), so the original failure is detected.\n\n## Production recovery\n\n1. **To restore availability immediately**, roll back only the Cloudflare Worker application to the last functioning v13 deployment using Workers & Pages -> your Worker -> Deployments -> prior stable version -> Rollback. This does not roll back D1 or R2 data. v13 ignores the extra v14 artist lookup table.\n2. Confirm `GET /api/peer` and `GET /api/tracks` work on `music.playy.online`, and test a playlist. Do not delete tables, restore an old database snapshot, or reset R2.\n3. Back up D1, deploy v14.0.1 to staging with a separate test D1 database, then stage with a production-like number of tracks. Test the full library, artist pages, playlists, reactions, and peer relay.\n4. Only after staging passes, deploy v14.0.1 to production. The failed v14 backfill should complete on a subsequent request once any expired migration lease is released. No manual D1 cleanup is necessary. If a failed migration left a lease, wait up to 90 seconds for it to expire and retry.\n5. Confirm `SELECT value FROM settings WHERE key = 'wasp_schema_version'` returns `14` and `/api/peer` returns HTTP 200. Watch D1 rows read and written after deployment.\n\n## Separate Docker incident\n\nA simultaneous Portainer rebuild failed with `no space left on device` during Docker image extraction. This is a Docker-host disk-space issue, **not** the D1 API failure. Check `df -h`, `sudo docker system df`, and `/var/lib/docker` capacity. You can usually reclaim unused build cache with `sudo docker builder prune` or expand the EC2 EBS filesystem. **Do not prune volumes or remove running containers**; preserve the persistent WASP database, music directories, and Portainer data. Fix Cloudflare availability first, then revisit Docker disk capacity.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Node/SQLite regression: 155 tracks, 310 artist mappings, max 90 bound parameters — pass.\n- Original v14 query/caching test — pass.\n- Existing JavaScript Worker API and gateway tests — pass.\n- **Not yet tested:** production D1 execution or complete Docker image build.\n"},{"name":"V13-WASP-UPDATE.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Branding and administration update\n\nThis package extends the working RiffVault v13 peer-relay project; it does not automatically deploy to any Cloudflare Worker. Preserve production D1/R2 bindings, and test separately before enabling deployment workflows.\n\n- `Admin → General settings → Branding`: choose the site name and whether to show its uploaded browser icon before the top-left name. Default WASP icon bundled in `public/wasp-icon.png`; upload a browser icon to override it.\n- Username registration matches the existing password input style.\n- Player and album `+` buttons present an authentication modal for guests with Register and Log in choices.\n- Artist biography, lineup and similar-artist data can be retrieved through an approved peer in Docker relay mode when the artist is not hosted locally.\n- `Admin → Users`: read-only registered user counts. Historical browser-anonymous reactions cannot be retroactively assigned to accounts; only future authenticated song reactions contribute to per-account counts.\n- Request form action spacing improved.\n\n**Security:** Any old default administrator password must be changed before exposing the application. Do not make your music library publicly shareable until you have set a unique strong password.\n\n## Deployment safety\n\n- Publish changes to a test branch/Portainer stack first; production Cloudflare is not updated by this archive.\n- The existing Docker service name, image name, Worker name and D1/R2 bindings are unchanged intentionally so data volumes and Cloudflare resources are not replaced.\n- Portainer Git stacks must rebuild the Docker image (not merely restart the old one) to include updated HTML, relay allowlist and Worker code.\n- The new `user_song_reactions` table is additive; no existing music files, reactions or playlists are deleted.\n- Browser icon visibility is opt-in under Admin → General settings.\n"},{"name":"V13-WASP-PEER-REACTIONS-SPOTIFY.md","content":"# WASP v13 — branding, local peer reactions, and playlist search\n\nThis project continues the WASP v13 Docker peer-relay build. The change is additive and does not require resetting the existing database or replacing music files.\n\n## What is included\n\n- `README.md` now opens with WASP branding and a **transparent-background** wasp mascot (`public/wasp-mascot.png`).\n- Logged-out visitors see **Sign In** next to the account icon. Account names appear after authentication.\n- Tracks streamed from registered peers support **Heart**, **Like**, and **Dislike**. Votes are stored on the listening WASP server in its own D1 database; they are **not** sent to the remote music host. The backend validates peer registration. Likes and dislikes are mutually exclusive. Authenticated user votes contribute to the local Users statistics going forward; earlier anonymous votes cannot be retroactively attributed.\n- Artist artwork falls back to other songs by that artist when the online artist image cannot load. When a Docker host has MP3s with embedded artwork, the existing `/art/track` handler supplies it during the fallback chain. Source music remains unchanged.\n- Under **Request → Make a request → Playlist**, hosts with Spotify API credentials can search Spotify playlist metadata and select a result, which fills the original Spotify playlist link field. Copy/paste links work without search credentials.\n\n## Spotify developer credentials\n\nCreate an app at https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard and obtain its Client ID and Client Secret. Spotify developer policies and account limitations apply. Some applications may not have access to the Spotify Search endpoint, and rate limits can apply. WASP shows an error and leaves the paste-link option available in that case.\n\n**Docker / Portainer:** Set `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID` and `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET` under the stack's environment variables, then recreate the container. The Docker start script writes a temporary, container-local `.dev.vars` file readable by Wrangler; it does not put the secret into an HTTP endpoint or browser bundle. Protect the Portainer admin interface and the server.\n\n**Local developer:** Create a gitignored `.dev.vars` beside `wrangler.jsonc` containing:\n\n```dotenv\nSPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=\"YOUR_CLIENT_ID\"\nSPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=\"YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET\"\n```\n\n**Cloudflare Worker:** Add `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID` and `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET` as Worker secrets using Cloudflare's dashboard or `npx wrangler secret put`. Do not commit secrets in Wrangler config or Git. Deploy to a staging Worker first, then explicitly promote your tested version.\n\nSpotify playlist search retrieves only playlist metadata. It does not copy or download Spotify audio; playlist requests continue through WASP's existing queue.\n\n## Tests\n\n```bash\nnode tests/wasp_v13_peer_spotify.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_wasp_v13_extra.py\n```\n\nThe existing Worker, Docker gateway, peer relay, and browser regression tests remain in `tests/`.\n\n## Deployment safety\n\nBack up existing Docker data volumes before redeploying the stack. Do not run `docker compose down -v` and do not share the default administrator password. No Cloudflare production data or running EC2 instances were changed by producing this source package.\n"},{"name":"V13-SPOTIFY-ADMIN-SETTINGS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Spotify settings in Admin → Requests\n\n## Administrator workflow\n\n1. Sign in as administrator and open **Admin → Requests → Spotify settings** (`/admin.html#requests/spotify`).\n2. Paste the Client ID and Client Secret from your Spotify Developer application.\n3. Select **Save credentials**. The Client Secret is never displayed again or included in subsequent API responses.\n4. Use **Request → Make a request → Playlist → Search** to look up public Spotify playlists. The existing paste-link field remains available.\n5. To change the Client ID but keep the existing encrypted secret, leave the secret input blank. To rotate it, enter a new secret.\n6. **Remove saved credentials** removes only the WASP database copy; it does not revoke credentials on Spotify or remove any environment-supplied values.\n\n## Encryption and deployment\n\n- Only authenticated administrators can call `GET`, `PUT`, and `DELETE /api/admin/spotify-config`.\n- Saved credentials use AES-GCM and a random IV. The encryption key comes from `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` or Docker's persisted `SCANNER_SECRET`; it is **not** stored with the encrypted credentials.\n- Docker creates its installation secret automatically and persists it in the application-state volume. Back up the volume. Avoid changing `SCANNER_SECRET` afterward.\n- For Cloudflare Workers, provision a persistent random `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` as a Worker secret **once** before using the new admin form. If it is missing, saving is refused with an explanation. The Worker environment-credential fallback remains supported.\n- Database-saved settings take precedence over environment-supplied Spotify credentials; deleting the saved settings restores the environment fallback.\n- If the encryption key changes, saved credentials cannot be decrypted. Restoring the previous key or entering new credentials with the current key resolves that condition.\n- Use HTTPS when entering credentials, and never share your Spotify secret or encryption key.\n- Spotify API access and search availability are ultimately subject to Spotify's permissions and policies. An API credential pair is not a guarantee of playlist search access.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nnode --experimental-sqlite tests/spotify_admin_config.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_spotify_admin.py\n```\n\nThese exercise admin authentication, encrypted storage, masked secrets, key rotation failure, token-cache invalidation, environment fallback, Requests subtab styling, navigation and save/remove actions.\n\nThis update has not been deployed to the user's live Cloudflare Worker or Docker host.\n"},{"name":"V13-SIMILAR-PEER-REACTIONS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Similar Artists and Local Peer Reactions\n\nThis package continues the v13 Docker peer-relay and Spotify administration release. It does **not** require changing your Tailscale settings or Cloudflare Worker just to use the fixes on a Docker host.\n\n## Similar Artists\n\nBoth the Similar Artists tab and the full `/#similar/<artist>` page now request recommendations through the same-origin relay registered for each connected peer. An empty response from one source no longer prevents another eligible peer from returning recommendations. These are actual responses supplied by a configured server's recommendation endpoint; when none return results, the UI still correctly reports that no similar artists were found.\n\nThe artist cards use precise availability labels:\n\n- **In your library**: this WASP host contains a track by the artist.\n- **Peer Library**: a connected peer contains a track by the artist, but this host does not. Clicking opens that artist's page and peer-hosted songs.\n- **Not in the library**: no song by that artist is found in the currently loaded local or connected-peer catalogue; clicking offers to request the artist.\n\nIf a peer's similar-artist provider cannot find recommendations (or its API is unavailable), WASP cannot manufacture results. The old local-only restriction in the full-page lookup has been corrected.\n\n## Reactions\n\n- Heart, Like and Dislike buttons remain available to guests and signed-in listeners for both local and peer tracks.\n- A click updates the visible count immediately (optimistically). The local Worker persists votes and returns the authoritative count; failures show an error and undo the optimistic update.\n- When a song is playing, the player periodically fetches only its reaction counts, including the current visitor's vote, so other listeners' votes become visible without refreshing the page.\n- Every peer song is identified by the configured peer origin **and** original track ID. The source peer's database and actual music files are never changed when a local user votes.\n- Visitor identity is preserved in the browser's local storage; signed-in user-linked reaction statistics are still recorded by the existing server logic. Anonymous browser data is not automatically merged into an account.\n\n## Deployment\n\n1. Back up the existing Docker data volumes.\n2. Update the **staging** GitHub repository from the full ZIP and have Portainer rebuild the image when redeploying.\n3. Keep any existing peer URL and `PEER_RELAY_ALIASES` settings in Portainer unchanged.\n4. Verify `/#similar/<peer artist>` on your Docker WASP instance and check that a **Peer Library** artist link opens its music.\n5. Play a peer track, click Heart, and confirm the count increments immediately. Open a second browser to check that it receives the saved count after its next refresh.\n\nDo not delete Docker volumes or overwrite production Cloudflare data. No new environment variables or destructive database migrations are required for these changes.\n\n## Automated checks\n\n- `node tests/v13_similar_reactions.test.mjs`\n- `python tests/ui_v13_similar_reactions.py`\n- Existing `tests/*.test.mjs`, `tests/api_smoke.mjs`, and `tests/ui*.py`\n\nA live Docker build and a live network-to-Cloudflare smoke test are still needed before calling this a stable production release.\n"},{"name":"V13-PLAYLIST-ARTIST-RATINGS-FIX.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Artist and playlist reaction rankings\n\nThis update is based on `WASP-v13-peer-reaction-charts-fix.zip`. It preserves the existing Docker, Cloudflare Worker, D1, R2, and peer-relay deployment configuration.\n\n## Artist rankings\n\nFixes the `libNames is not defined` exception on **Most hearted / Most liked / Most disliked → Artists**. Lists are ranked from reactions saved on the current WASP installation, including artists available only through approved connected peers. A changed artist vote does not modify the peer's original database.\n\n## Playlist ratings\n\nPlaylist detail pages now display **♡ Heart** (with its text label), an icon-only **Like** button, and an icon-only **Dislike** button. They work on the local server's playlists, public user playlists, and connected peers' shared playlists. Guests and signed-in users can vote with their existing per-visitor ID. Like and Dislike are mutually exclusive; Heart is independent. Counts update optimistically and the interface rolls back an unsuccessful save.\n\nThe playlist ranking pages combine server playlists, shared peer playlists, and public user playlists and use **this server's votes** for all three rankings. Peer playlist keys are scoped by source origin and remote playlist ID so an ID collision between servers does not combine votes. Minimal metadata for voted peer playlists is saved locally so their ranking entries remain recognizable while the peer is temporarily offline. The audio files are not copied, and playback still depends on the peer being reachable.\n\nExisting hearts saved through `/api/playlist-heart` are copied into the unified ranking table using an idempotent, non-destructive migration. The original heart table remains intact. A new `voted_playlist_cache` table holds peer playlist display metadata only. No music objects, tracks, existing playlists, or user accounts are deleted.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe bundled automated tests cover the artist ranking page, peer/local/public playlist voting, all three playlist ranking pages, unauthorized or unknown peer inputs, old hearts migration, peer source metadata, and the existing WASP regression suite.\n\nDeploy into staging first and rebuild the Docker image. Back up the persistent Docker state before updating. Do not use `docker compose down -v`. This package does not change the already-running Cloudflare production service or your Tailscale bridge. An end-to-end test on your live EC2 installation has **not** been performed.\n"},{"name":"V13-PEER-RELAY.md","content":"# RiffVault v13 — Docker peer relay\n\nRiffVault v13 keeps the v12 music library, accounts and playlists, and adds a **Docker-only same-origin relay** for a connected peer whose HTTP address is private or blocked from visitors' browsers.\n\n## How this works\n\nBrowser -> RiffVault Docker (same HTTP(S) origin) -> Node gateway -> connected peer URL (e.g. Tailscale Bridge/Nginx) -> Cloudflare/RiffVault source.\n\nOnly the Docker instance contacts the Tailscale address. A browser no longer fetches the peer catalogue, playlists, public-user playlist covers, or audio from a `100.x.x.x` URL. Audio remains on the source peer and is **streamed on demand** with HTTP byte-range support; it is not copied into the local music library.\n\nThe server only relays **read-only**, explicitly allowlisted public peer routes (`/api/tracks`, `/api/playlists`, `/api/user-playlists`, `/api/peer`, `/api/site`, `/art/...` and `/audio/<id>`). Only administrators can register remote peer servers. The relay strips session cookies/credentials and rejects off-site redirects; it is not a general-purpose network proxy.\n\n## Deploy from the working v12 installation through Portainer\n\n1. **Back up** the Docker data volume, user playlists, and current Git revision. Keep the existing v12 deployment available for rollback.\n2. Extract this ZIP into your **staging Git branch/repository** (the repository selected by Portainer). It has the original `docker-compose.yml` and all v12 sources with targeted v13 changes. Do not push to a Git branch that automatically redeploys the production Cloudflare Worker unless that is intentional.\n3. Commit and push the update. Under **Portainer -> Stacks -> riffvault**, use **Pull and redeploy**, rebuilding the Docker image. The v13 Compose file sets `pull_policy: build` to request a source rebuild. Confirm that the newly built image was deployed rather than merely restarting the old container.\n4. Keep the existing Portainer environment variables and Docker volumes. **Do not remove volumes or run `docker compose down -v`.** No database reset or destructive migration is required.\n5. In **Admin -> Connected peers**, add the Tailscale-accessible bridge URL, e.g. `http://100.107.221.71:8081`. The peer's `/api/peer` endpoint should report the shared music library, and the Bridge Nginx service must reach the real music host.\n6. Open RiffVault from the private-network browser; go to **Playlists -> Peer playlists** or the main music catalogue. Browser DevTools should show requests to `/_peer/<peer-id>/api/tracks` and `/_peer/<peer-id>/audio/<track-id>`, **not** direct requests to `100.107.221.71:8081`.\n\n> The most reliable production setup uses a trusted HTTPS domain for Docker RiffVault. Plain HTTP over the public internet exposes login information. Restrict the EC2/security group until the default administrator password has been changed.\n\n## Optional: canonical-origin alias for third-party playlist references\n\nIf a playlist on the bridge reports songs as hosted by the *original* Cloudflare URL (`https://music.playy.online`) rather than its *bridge* URL, configure the optional Portainer stack environment variable:\n\n```text\nPEER_RELAY_ALIASES={\"https://music.playy.online\":\"http://100.107.221.71:8081\"}\n```\n\nThis is a JSON object mapping **canonical public origins** to **already connected, admin-approved peer URLs**. It affects only how the browser selects the local relay for existing source references. It does not let clients choose arbitrary upstream websites. Redeploy the Docker container after changing it.\n\nThe alias is not needed for a normal playlist whose tracks are all directly hosted by the connected bridge. For songs hosted by unrelated third-party servers, add those source servers as approved peers rather than assuming they live behind this bridge.\n\n## Verify after the update\n\nOn the Docker EC2 host (container name `riffvault`):\n\n```bash\n# Must return 200 and a nonzero byte count\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}, %{size_download} bytes\\n' http://127.0.0.1:8080/\n\n# Confirm the registry includes the relay_base fields\ncurl -sS http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/network/peers\n\n# Verify the local gateway is running\nsudo docker exec riffvault node -e 'fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:8788/api/network/peers\").then(r=>r.text()).then(console.log).catch(console.error)'\n```\n\nTo test media playback in a browser, select a peer-hosted song, play it and seek. The Network panel should show an audio request on your **Docker RiffVault hostname**, with HTTP `200` or `206 Partial Content` depending on the request.\n\n## Known limitations and safety\n\n- **Cloudflare Workers production is not modified.** Docker handles the relay and keeps its D1/R2-emulated storage independent from Cloudflare production.\n- The source host must expose working `/audio` and `/art` routes. URLs to unrelated external image/CDN hosts are not automatically proxied; the existing nine-tile artwork fallback remains available.\n- If a peer goes offline, the relay returns a gateway error; it does not automatically download or permanently cache that peer's songs.\n- Relayed audio uses Docker/EC2 bandwidth and may incur AWS transfer costs. Access to a public RiffVault server can use the relay; apply appropriate server/network limits.\n- For browsers in restricted private networks, Tailscale itself does not need to be installed on the client. Do **not** change your Docker EC2's default route to a Tailscale exit node.\n- `curl /api/network/peers` succeeds even when the peer is offline because the registry is local. A working remote track request must be checked separately.\n\n## Run included isolated checks\n\n```bash\nnode --test tests/peer_relay.test.mjs tests/gateway_proxy.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_peer_relay_smoke.py\npython tests/ui_smoke.py\nnode tests/api_smoke.mjs\n```\n\nBrowser tests require Python Playwright with Chromium. The tests use local mock peers and do not touch actual Cloudflare music, accounts or databases.\n"},{"name":"V13-PEER-REACTION-CHARTS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Peer reactions in popularity charts\n\nThis update makes **Most hearted**, **Most liked**, and **Most disliked** include peer-hosted songs that receive votes on the current WASP instance.\n\n- Counts on a peer song represent votes stored **on this WASP server**, not votes submitted to the source peer.\n- Guest and signed-in user votes continue to work without uploading or deleting any remote audio.\n- Popularity charts combine matching recordings into one card when the local server and a connected peer have the same song.\n- The local server stores a small amount of track metadata alongside peer reactions, allowing a previously reacted-to song to remain visible in rankings while its peer catalogue is temporarily unavailable. Audio playback still requires a reachable source server.\n- Votes in the music player update immediately. Chart pages also refresh vote totals automatically while visible.\n- Existing database tables are preserved. Five optional columns are added to the existing `peer_reaction_tracks` table through additive migrations.\n- The browser still uses the configured same-origin peer relay. No Tailscale exit-node or Cloudflare production changes are required.\n\n## Deployment and testing\n\nUpdate your staging Git repository and **rebuild** the Docker image using Portainer. Keep existing Docker volumes. Do not use `docker compose down -v`.\n\nVerify by playing a song supplied by a connected peer, clicking Heart/Like/Dislike, and visiting `/#hearts`, `/#likes` or `/#dislikes`. The song should appear with the local vote count. You can repeat this in a private browsing window to verify guest voting. If the peer is temporarily unavailable, existing metadata and votes remain visible, but audio may not play.\n\nAutomated checks include `node tests/v13_peer_reaction_charts.test.mjs` and `python tests/ui_peer_reaction_charts.py`.\n\nThis is a staging build; it has not been deployed to the user's live EC2 or Cloudflare environment.\n"}];
